## IRMAA cliffs on the ordinary-income chart

### Sources checked before hardcoding anything

CMS's own fact sheet 403s WebFetch, so the 2025 schedule was cross-checked
against irmaahub.com and valuepenguin.com. They agree on everything except one
obvious typo (valuepenguin prints the tier-3 Part B *surcharge* as $259.90; it
is $295.90, since $480.90 - $185.00). Final table, keyed to 2023 MAGI:

  tier  single >   joint >    Part B/mo  Part B surch.  Part D surch.
  0     -          -          185.00     0              0
  1     106,000    212,000    259.00     74.00          13.70
  2     133,000    266,000    370.00     185.00         35.30
  3     167,000    334,000    480.90     295.90         57.00
  4     200,000    400,000    591.90     406.90         78.60
  5     500,000    750,000    628.90     443.90         85.80

The joint top tier is $750,000, NOT double $500,000 — irmaahub prints
$1,000,000 and is wrong. The $500k/$750k tier came from the Bipartisan Budget
Act of 2018 and is fixed in statute rather than indexed, so it never doubled.
The PLAN bullet's "double for MFJ except the top tier" was right; a source was
not. There is a test asserting exactly this.

### Numbers worth keeping (single, average benefit $23,712, no muni)

- Annualized Part B + Part D surcharge per enrollee: 1,052.40 / 2,643.60 /
  4,234.80 / 5,826.00 / 6,356.40. The *steps* are 1,052.40, then 1,591.20
  three times, then 530.40 — the three middle cliffs cost exactly the same to
  cross, which is a nicer fact than any of the levels.
- Cliff placement on the other-income axis: past the 85% cap the benefit is a
  fixed $20,155.20 of AGI, so every cliff sits exactly that much below its MAGI
  figure — 85,844.80 / 112,844.80 / 146,844.80 / 179,844.80 / 479,844.80. Only
  the first three fit inside MAX_INCOME = 150,000.
- Inside the torpedo the shift is worse than a constant: at the MAX benefit
  ($61,296) the 85% cap has not bound by $106,000 of MAGI, MAGI climbs at $1.85
  per dollar earned, and cliff 1 lands at $56,404.97 of other income instead of
  $85,844.80. Asserted with toBeCloseTo.
- MFJ: cliff 1 needs $212,000 of MAGI = $191,844.80 of other income, past the
  right edge of the chart. So a joint return draws NO reference lines at all and
  the section takes its "No cliff falls inside the chart above" branch. Do not
  treat that as a bug; there is a test for it.
- Muni interest shifts the *cliffs* left exactly 1:1 (it is in Medicare's MAGI
  and in provisional income, and past the cap the second channel is closed).
  But it moves the *scenario MAGI* by more than 1:1 when the cap has not bound:
  at the app default, $10,000 of muni takes MAGI 41,178 -> 59,678, not 51,178,
  because it also drags $8,500 more of benefits into AGI. My first draft of that
  App test asserted 51,178. Corrected.

### Implementation

tax.ts gained a self-contained IRMAA section placed *before* the Roth section,
because the pre-existing IRMAA_TIER1_MAGI is now spread from IRMAA_TIERS[1] and
conversionCeilings() reads it at module-eval time. New API: IRMAA_TIERS,
PART_B_STANDARD_PREMIUM, IRMAA_PREMIUM_YEAR/MAGI_YEAR/LOOKBACK_YEARS,
partBSurchargeMonthly, irmaaMagi, irmaaTierFor, irmaaFor, otherIncomeAtIrmaaMagi,
irmaaCliffs.

- Thresholds are exclusive (`magi > tier.magiOver`), because the statute charges
  the tier "in excess of" the amount. Tier 0 uses -Infinity as its floor.
- otherIncomeAtIrmaaMagi inverts MAGI by bisection over [0, targetMagi].
  targetMagi is always a valid upper bracket since MAGI >= other income. MAGI is
  continuous and strictly increasing (slope 1 / 1.5 / 1.85), so this is exact,
  not approximate. 60 iterations.
- All premium arithmetic goes through a toCents() rounder; 74 + 13.7 times 12
  is 1052.3999999999999 in raw float and the tests assert 1052.4 exactly.
- beneficiaries is a separate axis from `seniors`: Medicare is charged per
  enrollee, so MFJ + both-65 pays every surcharge twice off one MAGI. App
  derives it as `filingStatus === 'mfj' && seniors === 2 ? 2 : 1` — deliberately
  floored at 1 rather than 0, since the two-year lag means a 63-year-old's
  income today still sets their first premium.

App.tsx: ReferenceLines (rose, dashed, labelled "IRMAA n") for the cliffs inside
[0, MAX_INCOME]; chart top margin 10 -> 22 to make room for the labels and right
margin 10 -> 28 because cliff 3 sits at 97.9% of the axis and its label would
otherwise clip. CustomTooltip gained optional filingStatus/muniInterest/
beneficiaries props (optional so the four existing tooltip tests still pass
unchanged) and now always shows the annualized surcharge plus the MAGI room to
the next cliff. New rose section under the muni one: 5-tile stat grid, the full
premium table with the in-force row highlighted, and an explicit x-axis caveat —
2025 premiums are set by 2023 MAGI, so today's income is really setting the 2027
premium under a schedule CMS has not published, and Form SSA-44 appeals a
life-changing event rather than waiting the lag out.

### The one testing thing worth remembering

ResponsiveContainer measures its parent and jsdom reports every element as
zero-sized, so recharts renders NOTHING in the main App suite — no reference
lines to assert on. src/App.chart.test.tsx works around this by vi.mock'ing the
recharts module and re-exporting ResponsiveContainer wrapped at a fixed
800x400. That is the only way to test anything that lives inside the SVG. It
asserts the labels, the left-to-right ordering, the equal leftward shift when
muni is added, and that MFJ drops the lines entirely.

