## Married Filing Separately (lived with spouse)
## 2026-08-19 — recovered and committed by a later iteration

The iteration that wrote this finished the work, flipped the PLAN checkbox, and
then died before committing. This iteration found 8 modified files in the tree,
re-verified them from scratch (tsc -b, eslint, 170 vitest tests, vite build —
all clean) and committed them unchanged. The write-up below is reconstructed
from the diff, not from that iteration's own notes, so it records what the code
does rather than what it was thinking.

### The rule

IRC 86(c)(1)(C) and 86(c)(2)(C) set BOTH provisional-income base amounts to
zero for a married taxpayer who does not file jointly and does not live apart
from their spouse for the entire year. A $0 base and a $0 adjusted base leave
the 50% tier zero dollars wide, so the whole formula collapses to
min(0.85 * PI, 0.85 * benefit).

Consequences the tests pin down:
- 42.5% of the benefit is taxable at $0 of other income (PI = half the benefit,
  times 0.85).
- The 85% cap binds once other income reaches HALF the benefit.
- The inclusion slope is never 1.5x — it goes straight to 1.85x and then flat.
  There is no valley and no hump, just the ceiling, immediately.

A separate filer who lived apart for all twelve months is treated as unmarried
by 86(c) and should pick `single` instead. The UI says so.

### Everything else MFS touches

- Brackets: section 1(j)(2)(D)'s, identical to single until $375,800, where a
  separate return tops out at 37% while a single one still has 35% room.
- Standard deduction: the same $15,750 — section 63(c)(2) files both statuses
  under "any other case".
- Age-65 addition: $1,600, the married amount, not single's $2,000. (63(f)(1)(B)
  does let a separate filer claim a spouse's addition, but only if that spouse
  has zero gross income — impossible for a spouse drawing Social Security, so
  it is not modelled.)
- OBBBA senior deduction: NONE. 151(d)(5)(C)(v) requires a joint return from any
  married individual. Not halved — zero. SENIOR_DEDUCTION_PHASEOUT_START[mfs] is
  `null` rather than a number, and seniorDeductionPhaseoutEnd() now returns
  `number | null`, which forced every caller to handle the absent case.
- LTCG: 0% band is $48,350 (exactly half the joint band, which coincidentally
  equals single's). The 15% band is NOT half — half of $600,050 is $300,025 but
  Rev. Proc. 2024-40 prints $300,000, because each status inflates from its own
  base and rounds separately.

### The IRMAA wrinkle, which is the part worth remembering

A separate return that lived with the spouse does NOT get a halved joint
schedule. 42 U.S.C. 1395r(i)(3)(C) gives it a two-step schedule of its own:
nothing until $106,000, then straight to what everyone else calls tier 4, then
tier 5 at $394,000. Tiers 1-3 do not exist for it. So its first cliff costs the
whole $5,826/yr in one step instead of arriving in four.

Modelled by setting mfs to `Infinity` on tiers 1-3 and filtering with a new
`irmaaTiersFor(status)`. Everything downstream — irmaaTierFor, irmaaFor's
"next tier", irmaaCliffs' "previous tier" — now walks that filtered ladder
instead of indexing IRMAA_TIERS by `tier.tier`. That indexing was the real
bug risk: `IRMAA_TIERS[tier.tier - 1]` is tier 3 for a separate filer standing
on tier 4, when the tier actually below them is tier 0.

Second wrinkle: the separate-return top threshold is INCLUSIVE. The statute
says "equal to or greater than" at 1395r(i)(3)(C)(ii)(II) where every other
threshold says "greater than", and CMS reproduces the difference verbatim.
Hence `inclusiveFor?: FilingStatus[]` on the tier and a dedicated test.

IRMAA_TIER1_MAGI was renamed IRMAA_FIRST_CLIFF_MAGI, since "tier 1" is not the
first cliff for every status.

