## Multi-year projection — the frozen thresholds over 10–30 years
## 2026-08-20 — inherited a crashed iteration's working tree and finished it

`src/utils/projection.ts` + a "The thresholds never move" section at the
bottom of the page. 251 tests pass, was 233.

### What the section actually shows

One slider drives both the COLA and the indexing of brackets, deductions,
other income and tax-exempt interest, so the filer's *real* income never
changes. Everything moves together except IRC 86(c)'s $25,000/$32,000 (1983)
and $34,000/$44,000 (1993). At the defaults — single, $30,000 of other income,
the 2025 average benefit — the taxable share climbs 47.14% → 85% by 2035 and
real federal tax goes $1,853 → $4,277, which is 2.31x, with nothing about the
filer changing at all. Set other income to $10,000 and it starts at 0% taxable
and reaches 32.53% by 2044. That contrast is the whole section.

Two things break the smooth climb and both get a reference line: the first
required distribution, and the OBBBA senior deduction expiring after 2028.

### Design decisions worth remembering

- **`Scenario.projected`** is the new escape hatch in tax.ts. `year` can only
  name a year in `TAX_YEAR_PARAMS`, which is right everywhere except a
  projection whose point is to run past it. When `projected` is set it wins for
  brackets/deductions, and the new `scenarioYear()` reports its calendar year —
  which is what finally makes `seniorDeductionFor` able to enforce
  151(d)(5)(D)'s "taxable years beginning after December 31, 2028". Use
  `scenarioYear()`, not `year`, for anything that turns on the calendar.
- **Indexing rounds the increase, not the result.** IRC 1(f) rounds the
  increase down to the next lowest multiple of $50 ($25 for a separate return's
  rate tables, but *not* its standard deduction — 63(c)(4) is excluded from the
  substitution). So `indexAmount` adds a rounded increase to an unrounded base
  rather than rounding the product; those differ whenever the base is not
  itself a multiple of the step, and the 2025 single 12% top ($48,475) is not.
- **1959 is the one birth year with no settled answer.** SECURE 2.0 § 107
  assigns it *both* 73 and 75 — someone born in 1959 attains 72 after 2022 and
  73 before 2033 (so 73), and attains 74 after 2032 (so 75). The final regs
  left 1.401(a)(9)-2(b)(2)(v) [Reserved] and the proposed regs (REG-103529-23,
  89 FR 58886) would fill it with 73. Following the proposal, and the UI says
  so on that slider position. This is the one figure in the file that could
  still change.
- **Projections index forward from the start year's published figures**, even
  for 2026 where Rev. Proc. 2025-32 is already out. Deliberate: the chart
  answers "what if inflation runs at X%", and splicing one published year into
  the middle would put a kink in the curve the assumption cannot explain. Now
  documented on `projectYearParams`. Logged as a backlog item.
- **LTCG is excluded outright.** A one-off realisation repeated for thirty
  years is not a projection of anything; a test pins `ltcg` making no
  difference to any row. IRMAA is excluded too — its thresholds *are* indexed,
  so including it blurs the point instead of sharpening it.

### Four defects in the inherited working tree, all fixed here

The tree held an unfinished version from a crashed iteration (plus a leftover
`src/__probe.test.tsx` that wrote snapshots to /tmp — deleted). It typechecked,
linted and passed 233 tests. The bugs were all in prose the tests did not
reach, which is a useful reminder that a section this conditional needs its
branches walked, not just its arithmetic checked.

1. **`seniorDeductionEndsYear` conflated the phaseout with the expiry.** It was
   "first row with zero after a row with some", which also fires when income
   growth walks the filer off the end of the unindexed $75k–$175k phaseout
   range. Single filer, $147,000 of other income, $30,000 benefit: it returned
   **2026** and the UI drew a "Senior deduction ends" line there with prose
   blaming a statute that does not expire until 2029. Now pinned to
   `SENIOR_DEDUCTION_LAST_YEAR + 1`. The phaseout deliberately draws nothing:
   it lands on zero 6 cents at a time, continuously, so there is no step there
   to point at.
2. **The first-RMD stat quoted the applicable age with someone else's
   divisor.** "At {applicableAge}, off a … balance divided by
   {divisors[firstRmdRow.age]}" — fine while the filer reaches the age during
   the projection, wrong for anyone already past it. Born 1945: "At 72 …
   divided by 20.2", which is 80's divisor. Now quotes the actual age and adds
   "the applicable age of 72 passed before 2025".
3. **"The step at {year} is the first required minimum distribution"** when
   there is no step — same already-past-the-age case, where the distribution
   runs from the first row. And "SECURE 2.0 pushed that age to 72", which
   SECURE 2.0 did not do (SECURE 1.0 did). Both branched; the SECURE 2.0
   sentence is now gated on `applicableAge > RMD_AGE_BEFORE_SECURE_2` and
   pluralises its year count.
4. **"The second step, at 2029, is the OBBBA senior deduction expiring"** — the
   two steps are independent and 2029 comes *first* whenever the filer reaches
   65 before the applicable age (born 1959: RMD at 2032). Now "The other step"
   when there is an RMD and "The step at" when there is not.

Two more found while writing the tests, same class:

5. **A climb claimed at a 0% COLA.** "The taxable share climbs from 47.14% to
   47.14%" — the flat control case had no branch. It has one now, and it is
   worth having: at zero COLA the frozen thresholds cost the filer nothing,
   which is the cleanest possible statement of what the ratchet actually is.
6. **The climb was credited to inflation even when the RMD was the only thing
   moving.** At a 0% COLA with a balance the share still climbs, because the
   balance grows at 5% against a standing-still income. Now attributed
   correctly in all three combinations, and tested in all three.

