## Nine sliders above the two charts they move
## 2026-08-20

Found the working tree dirty at start: App.tsx, App.test.tsx and index.css
carried an unfinished implementation of the section-local-slider decision from
a previous iteration that never reached a commit. Verified it rather than
redoing it — `npx tsc -b`, `npm run lint`, `npx vitest run src/App.test.tsx`
(168 passed, up from 163) and `npm run build` are all clean — so this iteration
finished and shipped it.

### What the change decides

The Over Time tab stacks two sections that price the same retirement. Between
them they carried nine sliders — five in the projection, four in the sequencing
comparison — and both sets sat *below* the charts they move. Worse, the two
sections had to describe the same scenario twice, and the sequencing prose had
to say "set above" and point two screens up.

They are now one `.tab-inputs` block above both, under two legends: "The
horizon" (years, COLA, birth year) and "Your accounts" (IRA balance, growth,
brokerage, basis, Roth, spending). Every field note came with its slider, which
is why the RMD-age note and the published-figures note moved too — four of the
projection tests now read them off the input block rather than the section.

### What stayed put, and why that is the point

The backlog had this task queued behind an unresolved question: the lump-sum
section's two sliders sit *with* the section that reads them and it reads
better that way, which was an argument for moving the other sections' inputs
down rather than up. The change settles it on a different axis than "up or
down" — **shared inputs get hoisted, single-reader inputs stay local**. Nine
sliders that two sections both read go to the tab; the fill-to ceiling that
exactly one of three strategies reads stays in the sequencing section; the two
back-pay sliders that nothing outside the lump-sum section reads stay with it.
Hoisting those to a block above six tabs would have put two inputs in front of
five tabs that never look at them.

Three backlog bullets were the same decision seen from three sections, so all
three are marked: the sequencing five, the lump-sum two, and the decision
itself.

### One edit of my own

Reflowed the sequencing ceiling's field note. The WIP had corrected a bad
indent there and left a 98-column line behind; the prose in that file wraps
near 88.

### Flaky suite, and it is not this change

`npx vitest run` across all seven files failed three times out of four, always
the same way: one App.test.tsx test at random blows the default 5s timeout and
takes 17s, 96s or 296s while every other test in the run finishes under 700ms.
Different test each time — the tab-mounting one once, a head-of-household one
the next.

Stashed the working tree and ran the full suite twice at HEAD: one clean pass
at 19s, one 202s failure. So it predates this change and is not a regression.
`npx vitest run src/App.test.tsx` alone is clean and takes 20s every time,
which points at the seven parallel forks — 168 App renders each building a full
recharts tree on a 10-core box — rather than at any one test. Logged as
discovered work with the candidate fixes (raise `testTimeout`, cap
`maxWorkers`, or find what the stalled test waits on). CI runs deploy only, so
nothing is red today.

### Not verified

No visual check. The repo has no browser tooling and the change is a DOM move
plus 33 lines of CSS, so the fieldset/legend rendering is argued rather than
seen. `display: flex` on a fieldset does change how browsers lay out the
legend; the border is removed anyway, so the legend should fall in as the
column's first item.

