## 2026-08-21 — Task: Replace the tab strip with a linear step-by-step flow

Two tabs became three steps: **1 Your benefit**, **2 The tax torpedo**,
**3 Capital gains**.

### The decision the task asked for: mounted, not swapped

The steps stay mounted and the page scrolls. Three reasons, all written into
the `STEPS` doc comment so the next iteration does not re-litigate it:

- A step that has to be clicked into existence reads as optional. These are
  numbered 1-2-3; a reader who never clicks tab 2 has not skipped a subject,
  they have stopped halfway through the argument.
- Steps 2 and 3 quote figures set in step 1 ("Ordinary income $30,000 +
  $23,712 SS" under the LTCG chart, the benefit in the torpedo axis label).
  That only works if scrolling back to the input is possible.
- Printing and Ctrl-F now reach the whole page rather than the open panel.

What it costs is a long page, which is what the nav and the next-step boxes
are for — and it is the reason the next backlog item ("give every step the
same shape") matters more now than it did under tabs.

### Keyboard handling: kept, re-based on the toolbar pattern

`role="tablist"`/`role="tab"` was wrong the moment the panels stopped
swapping — a tab implies it controls whether its panel is showing. The nav is
now `role="toolbar"` with `aria-label="Steps"`, buttons carrying
`aria-current="step"`, and the same roving tabindex + ArrowLeft/ArrowRight +
wrap-at-both-ends handler the strip had (`onStepKeyDown`).

One deliberate split between the two paths:

- **Click** (nav item or next-step box) → `goToStep`: sets the current step,
  focuses the `<section tabIndex={-1}>`, then `scrollIntoView({behavior:
  'smooth'})`. Focus has to land *inside* the step or the next Tab press sends
  a keyboard reader back to the top of the page.
- **Arrow** → focus stays on the nav button and only the scroll moves. Moving
  focus into the section on the first arrow press would make the second press
  impossible.

`scrollIntoView` is optional-called (`?.scrollIntoView?.(…)`) because jsdom
does not implement it and every test would otherwise throw.

### The next-step box

`nextStepBox(fromIndex)` renders the box for `STEPS[fromIndex + 1]`, so the
last step gets `null` and the flow ends rather than looping. It names its
destination — "Next · Step 2 of 3 / The tax torpedo / Add the income that is
not Social Security…" — because a bare "Next" is worth nothing to a reader
deciding whether to keep going. The arrow is `aria-hidden`, so the accessible
name is the three spans of prose.

### What moved

- The **Other Ordinary Income** slider moved out of the shared scenario block
  and into step 2, above the chart whose x-axis it is. Everything else in the
  shared block (year, filing status, age, benefit, and the collapsed
  `advanced-inputs`) is step 1.
- The Capital Gains `<section className="explainer">` became step 3 itself:
  its `<h2>` is the step heading (keeping `section-heading-amber`), and its
  two loose `<p>`s picked up `step-prose`, which carries what `.explainer p`
  used to give them.
- The LTCG slider is still in step 1's advanced disclosure, two steps above
  the chart it drives. Deliberate: the "re-do capital gains as a share of
  income already entered" item three bullets down the backlog takes it out of
  there, and moving it twice is worse than moving it once.

### CSS

`.tabs`/`.tab`/`.tab-active`/`.tab-panel` are gone, replaced by `.step-nav`,
`.step-nav-item` (+ `.step-nav-number`, a numbered circle — the order is the
point), `.step-nav-current`, `.step`, `.step-kicker`, `.step-heading`,
`.step-intro`/`.step-prose` and the `.next-step` grid. The separating rule
moved from "under the strip" to `.step + .step`, since there is no strip
hanging a panel off itself any more. `.step:focus-visible` gets an offset ring
so a keyboard reader can see where `goToStep` put them; `.step:focus` is bare.

### Tests

`describe('tabs')` became `describe('the step flow')` — 12 cases: the numbered
nav in order, all three headings on screen at once (plus no `tabpanel`
anywhere), step 1 current at load, click → current + focus inside the section,
`aria-controls` wiring, walking forward through both boxes, the boxes naming
their destination and stopping at step 3, arrow keys wrapping both ways, focus
staying on the nav while arrowing, roving tabindex, non-arrow keys ignored,
and every input staying mounted across the walk. The `renderTab` helper is
gone — nothing needs opening any more — and the advanced-inputs
"keeps its values across tabs" case now walks the nav instead.

### Verified

`npx tsc -b`, `npx eslint src --max-warnings 0`, `npx vitest run` (7 files,
407 tests, green in 4.0s), `npm run build`. CSS bundle 9.41 kB → 11.15 kB.

### Logged, not fixed

The nav marks the step the reader last *asked* for, not the one they are
looking at — with everything mounted, scrolling past a step with the mouse
leaves `aria-current` behind. Added to Discovered Work with the scroll-spy
sketch and the open question of whether the nav should also be sticky.

## 2026-08-21 — Task: Give every step the same shape

chart → the one slider that says where on it you are → the collapsed
explainers → the box to the next step. Steps 2 and 3 now read that way; step 1
has no curve of its own, so the return it sets (year, filing status, age)
stands where the chart stands and the benefit slider follows it.

### What moved

- **Step 2.** The Other Ordinary Income slider went from above the chart to
  below it — under the axis label and the IRMAA key, so the chart and its two
  captions stay one block. Nothing else in the step changed order.
- **Step 3.** Was heading → prose → chart → axis label → prose, with no slider
  at all. Now heading → one-line intro → chart → axis label → the LTCG slider →
  a single `<details className="explainer">` ("Why the two effects stack")
  holding both of the old paragraphs verbatim. The intro is written to match
  step 2's ("The chart prices every … the slider says which one is yours").
- **The LTCG slider left the advanced disclosure.** Last iteration deliberately
  left it there, reasoning that the capital-gains rework three bullets down
  would move it and moving it twice was worse. But that rework changes what the
  slider *means* (a share of income already entered rather than an addition),
  not where it lives, and step 3 cannot have the shared shape without it. So it
  moved once, to its final home.

### Why the readout exists

Moving the slider under the chart exposed something the ordering had been
hiding: `plannedLtcg` fed nothing at all. Not the torpedo curve, not the gains
curve, not a tooltip — only its own value and the advanced summary line. It had
been wired to the Roth and IRMAA sections deleted two iterations ago. Putting a
dead control in the main flow, where the disclosure's own stated rule is that
anything visible must earn its place, was not acceptable.

So each of the two charted steps got a `.slider-readout` under its slider,
reading the drawn curve back at the reader's value: "You are here. At $30,000
of other income the next dollar is taxed at 22.2%." `pointAt(curve, axis,
value)` walks the ascending sweep for the last sample at or below the value —
sliders step $500, curves sample $250, so it is an exact hit in practice and
the `undefined` branch renders an em dash. Step 3's line adds a clause when the
rate is past 20%: nothing above the top statutory LTCG rate can be the gain
itself, so the excess is benefit being dragged in beside it.

This is prose where the next backlog item asks for a labelled dashed vertical.
They complement rather than duplicate — the line puts the reader on the chart,
the sentence says what it costs them there — and the item stays open.

### Also

- `advancedSet` is two entries now, so "All three at $0" → "Both at $0" and
  the field note lost its capital-gains clause.
- The QCD field note said the gift comes out of "the other income set above".
  That slider has been *below* the disclosure since the tabs became steps —
  stale before this change, not caused by it. Now reads "set in step 2", which
  survives the next reordering too.

### CSS

`.chart-slider` (a top margin, no bottom one — it reads as part of the chart,
not as a control in a stack) and `.slider-readout` / `.slider-readout strong`,
sized like a field note.

### Tests

New `describe('the shape every step shares')`, 5 cases. `landmarks(id)` lists
a step's `.chart-container` / range input / `<details>` / `.next-step` in DOM
order, drops anything nested inside a disclosure, and collapses runs — so step
2 is `['chart','slider','details','next']`, step 3 `['chart','slider',
'details']`, step 1 `['slider','details','next']`. Collapsing runs means adding
a fourth explainer to step 2 will not break it. Two more assert each readout
tracks its own slider. The advanced-inputs describe lost its LTCG assertions
and gained the inverse: the gain slider is *not* in the disclosure, and the
"on screen" rule is now two-pronged — an input earns its place by moving the
opening picture or by being a chart's position marker.

### Verified

`npx tsc -b`, `npx eslint src --max-warnings 0`, `npx vitest run` (7 files,
412 tests, green in 4.3s), `npm run build`. CSS bundle 11.15 kB → 11.32 kB.

### Logged, not fixed

`plannedLtcg` still reaches no chart. `marginalRateCurve` accepts an `ltcg`
field and step 2 passes it none; `CustomTooltip` hardcodes `ltcg: 0` into
`irmaaMagi`. So an $80,000 planned gain leaves the torpedo curve, the total-tax
figure and every IRMAA cliff line priced as if nothing had been realized — and
