## 2026-08-21 — Task: Say on the page that the link is the return

Topmost unchecked bullet of Backlog 4. HEAD clean and green before; 623 tests
green after, up from 616. No existing test touched.

### What went in

A `.answer-share` block at the close, between the eight figures and the
caveat: one sentence — "The address bar is this return. Every control on this
page rides in the link…" — a **Copy link to this return** button, and a live
region for what the copy did.

### The three decisions

**What gets copied: `location.href`, verbatim.** Not a URL rebuilt with
`#answer` on it, which was tempting since the button sits at the close and the
close is what is worth sending. Two things killed it. `stepFromHash` only
knows the four `#step-…` ids, so `#answer` would land a reader at the close
under a nav insisting they are on step 1 — the exact mismatch that function
exists to prevent — and the close belongs to no step, so there is no right
marking for it. The deciding argument is the fallback: the failure message
tells the reader to select the address bar instead, and that is only honest if
the button copies the address bar character for character.

**The fallback: no text field.** The bullet suspected it was more code than
the feature it backs up, and it is worse than that — a second copy of the
address on the page is a second thing to keep in step with the return, stale
the moment a slider moves, where the address bar never is. So: no clipboard at
mount (plain http, Safari before 13.1) draws no button at all, because a copy
button that cannot copy is worse than none and the sentence beside it is the
whole feature. A write that *rejects* at click time gets a message pointing at
the address bar. One thing to say, one place to look.

**`aria-live="polite"` rather than `role="status"`.** The link note at the top
of the page is already the page's one status region, and a second would make
`getByRole('status')` ambiguous for a screen reader and for the three existing
tests that query it. `role=status` is defined as polite + atomic, so writing
those two attributes directly gets the same announcement without the
collision. The region is mounted always and empty when idle — a message has to
land *in* a live region rather than arrive with it — and `:empty { display:
none }` keeps it out of the layout meanwhile.

### One thing the copy has to be told about

"Copied" is a claim about the clipboard, and it stops being true of what is on
screen the moment a slider moves. The reset lives in the existing
address-bar-sync effect, which already knows the return changed and has the
eleven-value dependency array; duplicating that array for a second effect
would have been the only alternative. Same reasoning as the link note's
Dismiss button, and the comment says so.

### Verified

- 7 new tests: placement between `.answer-figures` and `.answer-note`, the
  exact `location.href` on the clipboard, a round trip (copy at $120,000 MFJ →
  unmount → open the copied link → the sliders and the $143,712 total come
  back), the message clearing on the next slider move, the rejection path, no
  button under jsdom's absent clipboard, and the live-region attributes plus
  no second `role="status"`.
- `npx tsc -b`, `npx eslint src --max-warnings 0`, `npx vitest run` (9 files,
  623 tests), `npm run build`.
- No browser tooling in this repo, so the flex layout is read rather than
  seen; jsdom covers structure and text only.

### Not filed as discovered work

Nothing broken turned up. The chunk warning and the oxc/esbuild banner are
both already bullets further down Backlog 4.

## 2026-08-21 — Task: Fix the link note's box, which has never drawn

Not a backlog bullet. I picked up the topmost unchecked one — the print
stylesheet — and while reading `index.css` for the palette found a rule in it
that can never match. Per the loop's own rule, fixing that became the
iteration. The print bullet stays unchecked.

### The bug

d5bcf75 ("Put the return in the address bar") added the link note — the amber
box that says a link asked for a value this page could not show — and wrote
its CSS by typing over the rule that happened to be sitting there:

    -.hint-bubble .field-note {
    +.hint-bubble .link-note {
    +  border-left: 3px solid #f59e0b;
    +  background: rgba(245, 158, 11, 0.08);
    +  ...

The `.hint-bubble ` prefix came along for the ride. `.link-note` renders as a
direct child of `.card` (App.tsx:1548) and never inside a bubble, so the
entire box treatment — border, left rule, tint, 13px type, `#cbd5e1` — has
been dead since the day it landed. The note draws as unboxed body-size text
with an amber `<strong>` in it. Every one of its seven siblings
(`.link-note p`, `strong`, `ul`, `li + li`, `button`, `button:hover`) is
correctly unscoped, which is why nothing else looked wrong.

Second casualty in the same hunk: the rule that got typed over left its body
behind as a bare `.field-note { margin: 0 }`, 35 lines above the real
`.field-note { margin: 0.5rem 0 0 }` — so it was dead where it stood, and the
first field note inside the hint bubble picked up 8px of top margin the
bubble's own padding already provides. Restored to `.hint-bubble .field-note`.

While there, moved the `.link-note` block out from among the hint-bubble rules
under its own header. The physical interleaving is what made the slip possible
and would have made it possible again.

### The guard

A rule that cannot match is silent: nothing throws, nothing warns, the build
is green, and the only symptom is a box that does not draw. jsdom does not
apply the Vite-imported stylesheet, so no rendering test could have caught it.

`src/styles.test.tsx` instead reads `index.css` off disk, pulls every rule
prelude with `/[^{}]+(?=\{)/g` (which handles the `@media` nesting that a
naive split on `}` does not — my first draft got that wrong and extracted
nothing, which is what the `length > 0` assertion in it is there to catch),
keeps only the plain `.a .b` shape, renders `<App />` and requires each one to
match something.

Narrow on purpose. `:hover` and `:has()` describe states one render is not in,
and `>`/`+` depend on arrangements a default render cannot stand in for — so
those are excluded rather than overlooked. What is left is a plain claim about
nesting that `querySelector` settles outright, and it is exactly the shape you
get by editing the wrong half of a selector you copied. Today it checks one
selector, `.hint-bubble .field-note`, and passes.

Verified it reproduces: re-adding the `.hint-bubble ` prefix fails the test
with `expected [ '.hint-bubble .link-note' ] to deeply equal []`.

### Verified

- `npx tsc -b`, `npx eslint src --max-warnings 0`, `npx vitest run`
  (10 files, 624 tests — one new), `npm run build`.
- No existing test changed. The fix is CSS only, so the 623 that were green
  before are green for the same reasons.
- No browser here, so the box itself is read rather than seen; what is
  verifiable is that the selector now matches the element the page renders.

### Notes for the print iteration that this displaced

Reading the palette turned up three things the `@media print` block will have
to handle, worth writing down while they are fresh:

- **`h1` and `.section-heading-amber` print as blank space.** Both paint their
  text with a `linear-gradient` background and knock the glyphs out with
  `-webkit-text-fill-color: transparent`. A browser that does not print
  backgrounds prints nothing at all — so the page title and step 3's heading
  are the two overrides the printed page is unreadable without.
- **The filing-status selection prints as unmarked.** `.segmented-option
  input:checked + span` shows the choice with a `background`, and the radio
  itself is `opacity: 0`. On paper the checked state needs a border or a
  weight, not a fill.
- **`.state-select` is `color: white`** on a dark ground — white on white.

Also: `.chart-container` is a fixed 400px holding a recharts SVG sized to the
screen, and recharts will not re-measure for print. Scaling it needs the
`viewBox` the SVG already carries plus `width: 100% !important; height: auto`
on `.recharts-wrapper` and its `svg`.

## 2026-08-21 — Task: Make the page printable (`@media print`)

Backlog 4, topmost unchecked — the bullet the previous iteration displaced
when it stopped to fix the link note's box. Its closing notes named four
things to handle and all four are handled here.

### The two decisions the bullet asked for

**The whole walk prints, not only the close.** The close was the tempting
answer: it restates the return and holds every figure a reader leaves with, so
it is already shaped like a one-page handout. But on its own it is a table of
numbers, and the charts with the reader's own marker standing on them are the
one part of this page that no amount of prose reproduces — they are what makes
those numbers mean something to whoever is handed the sheet. So the walk
prints, and `.answer` gets `break-before: page` so the handoff is still one
clean page for a reader who only wants that.

**An explainer prints exactly as the reader left it.** No `beforeprint`. The
listener pair the bullet describes buys roughly a doubling of the page count
in reference prose that is word-for-word identical for every reader, in
exchange for disclosure state the page would then have to remember and put
back — and `afterprint` does not fire reliably when a print is cancelled, so
the restore is not even guaranteed. What is on screen is what prints.

A *closed* explainer is hidden outright (`details.explainer:not([open])`)
rather than printed as its own heading: a heading with nothing under it reads
as a fault on paper. `.advanced-inputs` is the deliberate exception and prints
closed, because its summary carries what has been set — "Both at $0", or the
two figures. That is the whole distinction: an explainer's summary asks a
question, the advanced summary answers one about the return being printed.

### What the block covers

- **The ground.** `color-scheme: light`, black on white, the body gradient and
  the card's tint/blur/shadow/border/padding all off, `#root` unpadded.
- **The two gradient headings** the last iteration's notes flagged: `h1` and
  `.section-heading-amber` paint text with a `linear-gradient` and knock the
  glyphs out with `-webkit-text-fill-color: transparent`, so a browser
  printing without background graphics prints the knockout and not the paint.
  Both come back as solid colour. This is the one thing here with a test.
- **Text**, in the two groups the dark palette already has: what was set
  bright to carry against slate (`#f8fafc`/`#e2e8f0`/`#cbd5e1`) goes black,
  what was set slate to recede (`#94a3b8`/`#64748b`) goes `#333`. The accents
  are darkened rather than dropped — amber `#b45309`, sky `#0369a1`, rose
  `#be123c`, emerald `#047857`, violet `#6d28d9`, lime `#4d7c0f` — because
  each one ties a figure to a line on a chart or to the box it sits in, so the
  hue is information, not decoration.
- **Boxes.** Every tinted panel is an rgba wash a browser drops when background
  graphics are off, so the wash goes and the border carries them, solid.
- **The filing-status selection**, the second of the last iteration's flags:
  the radio is `opacity: 0` and the choice is shown with a fill, so on paper
  it was unmarked. Border and weight instead — and the border is subtracted
  from the padding, so marking the selection cannot be what wraps the last
  option onto a second row.
- **`.state-select`**, the third flag: white on a dark ground, plus a chevron
  drawn in two gradients that only means something next to a menu that opens.
- **Interactive-only chrome hidden**: the step nav, the next-step buttons, the
  copy-link button, the link note's dismiss button, both disclosure chevrons.
  Also the range inputs and their min/max labels — a track with no thumb says
  nothing that `.slider-header`'s own label and figure do not say in words,
  and the chart carries the position.
- **The charts**, the fourth flag. `.chart-container` is a fixed 400px holding
  a recharts SVG sized in pixels off a screen measurement recharts will not
  repeat for the printer, so a plot laid out for a 900px column overflows a
  6.5in page. `width: 100% !important; height: auto` on the container, the
  responsive container and the wrapper, plus the same on the surface `<svg>` —
  the `viewBox` recharts already writes is what makes that scale rather than
  crop. The `CartesianGrid` is white at 10% alpha, which on paper is nothing
  at all, so it and the axis stroke get printable greys.
- **Pagination.** `break-after: avoid` on headings and kickers, `break-inside:
  avoid` on each chart figure and each answer figure, `break-before: page` on
  the close.

### The recharts selectors are `>`-rooted on purpose

`.chart-container .recharts-wrapper` would be a dead selector to
`styles.test.tsx`'s nested-class guard: recharts renders *nothing* under jsdom,
because `ResponsiveContainer` measures its parent as zero (which is why
`App.chart.test.tsx` mocks it with a fixed 800x400). Rooting those rules with
`>` puts them in the child-arrangement case that guard already documents
itself as leaving out, rather than in a hole it does not know about. Said so
in the CSS.

### The guard

A print rule is invisible until someone prints. The sharpest mechanical
invariant in what was found is the gradient-heading one, so that is what is
pinned: **every selector in the stylesheet that sets
`-webkit-text-fill-color: transparent` must be given its colour back inside
`@media print`.** A third gradient heading fails the suite until it is.

`styles.test.tsx` gained a `leafRules` extractor — `/([^{}]+)\{([^{}]*)\}/g`,
which yields only innermost rules, because `[^{}]*` cannot span a nested `{`
so an `@media` prelude never completes a match and the scan walks past it to
the rules inside — and a brace-matching `printBlock`. The existing
nested-class test is untouched.

Verified it reproduces, twice: dropping the reset fails with
`expected [ 'h1', '.section-heading-amber' ] to deeply equal []`, and adding a
third gradient heading with no print reset fails with
`expected [ '.section-heading-rose' ] to deeply equal []`.

### Caught while writing it

`margin-top: 1rem` was in the shared sizing rule with the two recharts divs,
where it is space *inside* the plot rather than above it — 2rem of it. Split
onto `.chart-container` alone before committing.

### Verified

- `npx tsc -b`, `npx eslint src --max-warnings 0`, `npx vitest run` (10 files,
  625 tests — one new), `npm run build`.
- Read the minified `dist` CSS back to confirm the whole block survives the
  build intact and parses: `@page`, the `:not([open])`, the `>`-rooted
  recharts rules, `break-before: page` and the `[type=radio]:checked` rule all
  present (the minifier only normalises the attribute quotes).
- No browser in this repo, so the printed page is reasoned about rather than
  seen. What is verifiable here is that the rules parse, survive the build,
  and name elements the page renders.

### Discovered work

One new bullet, logged in PLAN.md: the reference-line labels print in their
line's own colour at 11px, which is 2–3:1 on white. They are inline SVG `fill`
attributes written per chart in App.tsx, so CSS can only flatten all of them
to one colour — which would cost the tie between each label and the line it
names. Left alone deliberately; it wants a decision in App.tsx, not a rule.

## 2026-08-21 — Task: No readout on the page is a live region

Every readout here was silent. A range input announces its own new value and
nothing else, so the "you are here" sentence, the advice paragraph under it,
the effective rate and all seven closing figures changed under a screen reader
without a word. `aria-live` appeared once on the page — the copy-link status
added last iteration — and `role="status"` once, on the link warning.

### The decision the bullet left open: how much to announce

The bullet's own warning is the design constraint. A polite region reads each
change out *in full* before it looks at the next, so the size of the reading
and the rate of change multiply: seven closing figures × sixty notches of a
drag is not an improvement on silence.

Three calls came out of that:

- **One region, not four.** Four regions — one per step — look right until you
  notice that step 1's benefit slider moves all four readings. One drag would
  queue four announcements. So there is a single region, and what it carries is
  the reading of the step whose control was last touched: `announceFrom`, a
  `StepId | null` that every control sets in its own `onChange`.
- **Keyed to the control, not to the nav.** Every step is mounted at once, so a
  reader can be working step 3's slider with the nav still marking step 1.
- **Debounced, not throttled.** A throttle reads out the middle of a drag,
  which is a figure the reader was passing *through* rather than one they
  chose. `useSettledReading` takes a new value only once it has held still for
  `READING_SETTLE_MS` (700), and drops everything before the last one.

Nothing is announced on arrival, which the bullet asked for and which falls out
of `announceFrom` starting null: the region renders empty, and content already
inside a live region at load is never announced. The visible page never waits
on any of this — only what the page *says* is delayed.

### What each reading says

One step's own readout, rewritten to be listened to rather than looked at:
plain sentences, no markup to flatten, no em dashes, figures in the order the
eye takes them off the page.

- **benefit** — the recap that closes step 1. It names the advanced inputs,
  where the recap on screen only points at them ("plus whatever is set under
  Advanced inputs"), because a pointer is no use to someone who has just moved
  one.
- **torpedo** — income, marginal rate, total tax, effective rate, then the
  standing headline.
- **gains** — the split, the rate on the next dollar of gain, tax and effective
  rate on the same total income.
- **conversion** — what fits under the chosen line and what it costs, or which
  of the two ways nothing fits.

### `standingHeadline`

The advice paragraph is five sentences of arithmetic: the right length to read,
the wrong length to hear on every notch. So its opening sentence was lifted out
into `standingHeadline(standing)` and both call it — `StandingNote` renders it
inside the `<strong>` each branch already had, and the torpedo reading appends
it. The branches select on the same conditions in the same order, so a sixth
position cannot be added to one and not the other. The existing "moves the
reader through all four positions on one curve" test already asserts on that
`<strong>`, so the refactor was covered before it was made.

### The state selector is deliberately left out

It moves no figure — it selects the footnote under step 2's chart — and a
`<select>` announces the option it lands on, so the only thing left to read
would be the whole footnote. Said so at the `onChange`, and logged in PLAN.md's
Discovered Work as the decision it is rather than the omission it looks like.

### Placement

Above the steps, not below them. Where a live region sits changes nothing about
when it is read, and the close is the last thing on this page before the
disclaimer — an invariant `the closing answer` already asserts
(`answer().nextElementSibling?.tagName === 'FOOTER'`). Putting the region at the
end broke that test, which was the test doing its job.

### Hiding it

`.live-reading` is clipped rather than hidden. Neither `display: none` nor
`visibility: hidden` will do — both take the element out of the accessibility
tree along with the layout, and a region that is not in the tree is never read.

### Verified

- `npx tsc -b`, `npx eslint src --max-warnings 0`, `npx vitest run`
  (10 files, 634 tests — nine new), `npm run build`, and read `.live-reading`
  back out of the minified `dist` CSS.
- Both mechanical claims falsified once each, to check the tests bite:
  dropping the `clearTimeout` in the hook fails "reads the end of a drag rather
  than every notch of it"; pointing the benefit slider at `'torpedo'` fails
  "carries the reading of the step whose control moved, and only that one".
- The new describe uses full fake timers where the rest of the file fakes only
  the clock — the settle delay is the subject, so it is advanced rather than
  waited out. `READING_SETTLE_MS` is exported for that.
- No screen reader in this repo. What is verifiable here is the markup, the
  timing and the text; that VoiceOver or NVDA reads it as intended is reasoned
  about rather than heard.

### Discovered work

One new bullet in PLAN.md: the state selector's silence, and whether a reading
that *summarises* rather than repeats belongs in a region whose premise so far
is that it says what the page says.

## 2026-08-21 — Task: Land the 400% FPL cliff, which was written and never committed

### What this iteration found

`git status` opened dirty: eight files modified, ~1,300 lines added, and
`ralph/PLAN.md` carrying a new hand-written "Backlog 5" list whose first bullet,
`add 400% FPL similar to IRMAA`, was already flipped to `[x]`. HEAD was
`2411bae` ("Say the readout out loud"), in sync with `origin/dev`, and its log
entry was the last one in this file. So a previous iteration did the whole FPL
task, marked it done, and exited — or was cut off — before committing, pushing,
or writing anything here.

That made landing it this iteration's task. An uncommitted tree is the one state
this loop cannot survive: the next run would have read a `[x]` bullet, seen no
commit, and had no way to tell finished work from abandoned work.

### What the orphaned change is

The 400% poverty-line cliff of IRC 36B, drawn on step 2's chart beside the IRMAA
cliffs it rhymes with. Read out of the diff rather than written here:

- **`src/utils/tax.ts` (+377).** `FPL_YEAR_PARAMS`, its own table for the same
  reason `IRMAA_YEAR_PARAMS` is — the guidelines come from a January HHS notice
  and the percentages from a summer Rev. Proc., neither of which is the autumn
  one that sets the brackets. `acaMagi` is the page's fourth MAGI and the widest:
  36B(d)(2)(B) adds the *untaxed* part of the benefit back, which undoes the
  torpedo, so this line rises a flat dollar per dollar of other income where
  Medicare's rises by up to $1.85. `otherIncomeAtAcaMagi` is the third solver
  alongside `otherIncomeAtAgi` and `otherIncomeAtIrmaaMagi`, still solved rather
  than rearranged even though its function is a straight line — the charitable
  exclusion puts a flat stretch at the left end, and three solvers that agree in
  form are easier to trust than two that agree and one that is clever.
  `ptcCliff` / `ptcFor` / `povertyLine` / `fplMultipleOf` answer the arithmetic;
  `Scenario.householdSize` is plumbed through `resolveScenario` with
  `defaultHouseholdSize`. A seventh `ConversionMeasure`, `'acaMagi'`, gives step
  4 a ceiling at the line.
- **`src/App.tsx` (+274).** `preMedicare` — 36B(c)(2)(B) makes anyone eligible
  for Medicare ineligible for the credit, so the line is drawn only for a return
  where someone has not turned 65. A fuchsia `ReferenceLine` labelled `400% FPL`,
  its own key entry beside the rose IRMAA one, and an explainer that states the
  reader's own multiple of the line.
- **`src/index.css` (+22).** `.chart-key-subsidy` on screen and again inside the
  `@media print` block, written *after* the rose rule because they share
  specificity.
- **README.md (+31).** Both the step list and the "what it models" paragraph.

Three honest omissions are written into the source rather than left silent: the
Alaska and Hawaii guidelines (one table, not three — the contiguous figure puts
the line to the *left* of theirs, which is the conservative direction), the
household size the page never asks for, and what crossing the line actually
costs, which is the benchmark silver premium for an age and a county this app
cannot know. Each already has its own bullet under Discovered Work.

The 2025 row carries `cliff: false`. ARPA section 9661, extended through 2025 by
IRA section 12001, capped a household's share at 8.5% of income with no upper
bound — so there is no cliff to draw on a 2025 return and there is one on a 2026
return, which is why the year is a flag rather than an assumption.

### Verified before committing

Nothing here was taken on the strength of the `[x]`:

- `npx tsc -b` — clean.
