## 2026-08-21 — Task: Ground the palette and the shape (visual overhaul, step 1 of 6)

### Breaking the bullet up first

`update visuals and structure. i like the look of ficalc` says in its own
wording to break itself into steps, so that came first and is now six
sub-bullets indented under it in PLAN.md. The parent stays `[ ]` until they
are all struck; a future iteration reading "topmost first" should land on the
parent, see the sub-list, and take the topmost unchecked child.

### What FI Calc actually looks like

Worth writing down because I guessed wrong before checking. I assumed a light
theme. It is not: `curl`ing `ficalc.app/assets/index-FkTiL_43.css` gives

    --fi-bg-color: #1a1a1b   --fi-font-color: #9799ae
    --border-color: #343940  --accentColor: #0270e8  --primaryBlue: #409dff
    --maxAppWidth: 44rem     --configuration-width: 18rem
    --chartFontSize: 13px    --chartLineWidth: 3px

and the shape falls out of the same file: `border-radius` is 4/6/8px and never
more; the only `box-shadow`s in 61KB are three focus rings and one modal; the
font stack is `system-ui`, no webfont; body copy is `.9375rem` and the type
scale tops out at 1.4rem.

So the difference from this page is not dark-vs-light. It is flat-vs-glass.
This page was slate-900 under a radial gradient with a frosted card floating on
it — `backdrop-filter: blur(12px)`, a 1.5rem radius, a 32px drop shadow — and
sky-400 accenting. FI Calc is near-black, hairline-bordered, small-cornered,
and accents in one blue.

### What this step did

**One token layer, and a test that keeps it the only one.** 51 distinct colour
literals were spread across 1,300 lines of `index.css` and 19 more across
`App.tsx`. They are now 36 custom properties in `:root` plus `src/palette.ts`,
which is the same palette in the one form an SVG attribute can read.

Two invariants in `styles.test.tsx` hold it there:

- `the screen stylesheet` — no colour literal outside `:root`, in the screen
  half of the sheet. Written as "an `rgb(`/`rgba(` whose first argument is a
  number", so `rgba(var(--accent-rgb), 0.2)` passes and `rgba(56, 189, 248,
  0.2)` does not. Keywords are left alone: `transparent` and `currentColor`
  name a relationship, not a colour.
- `the palette` — `palette.ts` and `:root` agree token for token. Two copies
  drift silently, because a stale colour still renders.

`palette.ts` holds literals rather than `var(--accent)` because jsdom does not
resolve custom properties in SVG attributes: every chart test would then be
asserting on the string `var(--accent)`, which cannot tell a right colour from
a wrong one.

**The print half is excluded from all of it, on purpose.** That block is a
*light* palette — `#000` on `#fff`, the accents darkened for paper — so it is
not a candidate for the dark tokens and re-pointing `--surface` to white would
have dragged the whole ink ramp with it. Its header comment was stale about the
radial gradient and now is not.

**Contrast went up, not down.** Every ink tier was measured against the new
`--surface` before it was chosen. Four of five land within 2 points of where
they were on slate; the fifth, `--ink-dim`, was 3.75:1 against the old
background — under AA for the footnotes, range labels and step kickers that use
it — and is 4.72:1 now. The two App-only colours folded into existing tokens on
the same grounds: `#ea580c` read 4.52:1 on the tooltip and `--orange` reads
5.74:1, and `#c084fc` was `--violet` to within a rounding error.

**The data colours did not move.** Amber is the marker, rose is a Medicare
cliff, fuchsia is the subsidy cliff, indigo is the conversion band, and a
reader matches a swatch in the key to a line in the SVG by hue alone. Renaming
them is this step; recolouring them is not, and the charts step is where the
SVG moves with them.

**The card stopped being a card.** It was a pane of frosted glass on a
gradient. The gradient is gone, so a bordered box drawn in the page's own
colour is just a line. What is left is its padding, which is all it was ever
doing for the reader.

### Verified

- `npx tsc -b`, `npx eslint src --max-warnings 0`, `npx vitest run`
  (10 files, 667 tests — two new), `npm run build`, and read the tokens back
  out of the minified `dist` CSS.
- Both new invariants falsified once each to check they bite: pasting `#94a3b8`
  back into `.field-note` fails `the screen stylesheet`; typoing `--accent` to
  `#3f9dff` in `palette.ts` alone fails `the palette`.
- Rendered rather than reasoned about: built, `vite preview`, and screenshotted
  the hero and step 2's chart through Playwright's cached Chromium at 2x. Zero
  page errors, and the chart reads correctly — flat plot, hairline grid, the
  new blue curve, and the amber/rose/fuchsia markers unchanged against it.
  (`playwright-core` was installed with `--no-save`; package.json and the
  lockfile are untouched.)
- The one existing test that asserts on colours — `['#f59e0b', '#34d399',
  '#f59e0b']` in `App.chart.test.tsx` — still passes, which is the check that
  the data hues really did stay put.

### Left standing, and why

The `h1` and step 3's heading still paint themselves with a `linear-gradient`
and knock the glyphs out. Both gradients now run accent-to-indigo, which is two
blues a step apart rather than the sky-to-indigo it was — visibly duller, and
not worth a spot fix, because the next sub-step deletes both gradients outright
along with the `@media print` rules that exist only to undo them.

The 618KB chunk warning is unchanged and is still its own Backlog 4 bullet.

## 2026-08-21 — Task: Bring the type down to FI Calc's scale (visual overhaul, step 2 of 6)

### The scale, read rather than guessed

The bullet says FI Calc's "headings stop around 1.4rem". That is `.secondaryHeader`
— and `.secondaryHeader`, `.primaryHeader` and `.page_title` appear zero times in
`index-Dn0nig3p.js`. They are dead rules in the stylesheet, left over from a page
the app no longer builds. Cross-referencing every CSS rule against the shipped
bundle gives the scale the running app actually spends:

    1.75rem / 900   .results_headerH1     (1.5rem under 550px)
    1.35rem / 700   .results_h2
    1.25rem         .configuration_title, .userPrefs_h2
    1.125rem / 700  .modalForm_h2, .glidePath_header
    .9375rem        body

Nothing live is larger than that h1. So the target for our own h1 is 1.75rem at
900, not 1.4rem — and 1.75rem is, by coincidence, exactly what our *step*
headings were set at, which is the clearest statement of how far the page was
over-scaled: its second-level heading was as large as FI Calc's first.

### What moved

    h1              2.5 / 800  →  1.75 / 900, 1.5 on a narrow screen
    .step-heading   1.75 / 700 →  1.35 / 700
    .answer-heading 1.25 / 700 →  1.35 / 700
    .explainer h2   1.5  / 700 →  1.125 / 700

The close's heading went *up*. It is the fourth section on the page, not a figure
inside one, so it belongs on the same step as the three step headings; it had been
sharing a size with the seven closing figures under it. Body copy was already
.9375rem and did not move.

Both headings also picked up an explicit `line-height` (1.2, 1.25). `:root` sets
1.5 for prose and FI Calc's global is normalize's 1.15 — a 28px heading set at 1.5
carries 14px of air it has no use for.

### Both gradients, and the print rules that existed to undo them

`h1` painted accent-to-indigo and `.section-heading-amber` painted amber-to-orange,
both knocking the glyphs out with `-webkit-text-fill-color: transparent`. Both are
gone: FI Calc paints no text with a gradient, and the knockout was a live print bug
— a browser dropping background graphics prints the knockout without the paint, so
the two loudest lines on the page came out of the printer as blank space. Three
print rules existed only to repair that and are deleted with it. `h1` keeps its one
print declaration, `color: #0c4a6e`; its 1.75rem needs no print override, where
2.5rem did.

`.section-heading-amber` is deleted outright rather than recoloured, and step 3's
heading is now plain `--ink-bright` like the other three. The amber signal that
ties that step to its curve survives where it is doing work — the slider value, the
chart key, the curve itself — and a single coloured heading among four was the
inconsistency, not the tie.

### The test that replaced the print pairing

`the print stylesheet` asserted that every gradient-knocked-out heading gets its
colour back inside `@media print`, and it opened with
`expect(knockedOut.length).toBeGreaterThan(0)` — a vacuity guard that now fails by
construction, because there are no knockouts left. Replaced by `the headings`,
which claims the stronger thing: nothing anywhere paints text with
`background-clip: text` or a transparent fill, so there is nothing for print to
repair. That shape stays true as headings are added; the old one had to be
re-satisfied by each new one.

`printBlock`, its brace-matching helper, had no other caller and went with it.

### And a test that pins the scale

`the type scale` holds a closed list of ten steps — .75, .8125, .875, .9375, 1,
1.125, 1.25, 1.35, 1.5, 1.75 — and fails on any size in the screen half that is not
one of them. A second `it` fails on any step declared but unspent, so the list
cannot rot into a superset of what the page uses.

This is the direction the failure actually runs. A scale does not drift by someone
rewriting it; it drifts by a 1.05rem typed into the one rule being edited, because
from inside that rule there is nothing to compare against. Now there is, and
widening the scale is an edit to the list, made once and on purpose. The scale is
also written out as a comment above `body`, where the 15px base is set.

### Verified

- `npx tsc -b`, `npx eslint src --max-warnings 0`, `npx vitest run`
  (10 files, 669 tests — net +2: one print test retired, three added),
  `npm run build`, tokens read back out of the minified `dist` CSS
  (`text-fill-color` count: 0).
- All three new invariants falsified once each: a gradient pasted onto
  `.answer-heading` fails `the headings`; `font-size: 1.05rem` fails `sets every
  size from one closed list of steps`; removing both 1.5rem uses fails `spends
  every step it declares`.
- Rendered rather than reasoned about. Built, `vite preview`, and drove Chromium
  through `playwright-core`: computed sizes are 28px/900 for the h1, 21.6px for
  every step heading and the close's, 18px for an explainer's, 24px for the h1 at
  a 500px viewport, and `webkitTextFillColor` equals `color` everywhere. Under
  `emulateMedia({ media: 'print' })` the h1 is `rgb(12, 74, 110)` with
  `background-image: none` and step 3's heading is black — which is the check that
  deleting `.section-heading-amber` left it inheriting the right print rule.
  Zero page errors. Screenshotted the hero, step 3, the explainer stack and the
  close.
- Note for a future iteration: the cached Chromium is build 1228 but the
  `playwright-core` in `node_modules` wants 1234, so `chromium.launch()` fails
  with "Executable doesn't exist". Passing `executablePath` at
  `~/Library/Caches/ms-playwright/chromium_headless_shell-1228/chrome-headless-shell-mac-arm64/chrome-headless-shell`
  works and needs no download. The script has to live in the project root, not
  `/tmp`, for the bare `playwright-core` import to resolve.

### Left standing, and why

The close and the next-step boxes are still accent-tinted panels on a bluish
raised surface, which now reads as the one place the page is not flat. That is the
sixth sub-bullet's job, not this one's. `--orange` is no longer spent by any CSS
rule — the amber gradient was its only use there — but it stays in `:root` because
`palette.ts` spends it on the tooltip and `the palette` requires the two to agree.

## 2026-08-21 — Task: Four sites printed `\u2014` at the reader (bug from a previous iteration)

Not a backlog bullet. The run opened with a dirty tree — `46a7448` was HEAD and in
sync, and `src/App.tsx` held seven uncommitted line changes and nothing else, no
test, no log entry. Reading the diff: it replaced `\u2014` with a real em dash in
four places. That is a fix, not a half-finished edit, so this iteration's job was
to finish it — verify it, cover it, and land it.

### What the bug was

`\u2014` is an em dash inside a JavaScript string and six literal characters
inside JSX text. Nothing at the call site tells them apart, and the page has both
spellings a few lines from each other: `'...prices \u2014 the year...'` at
App.tsx:145 is a single-quoted string and correct, while
`<option value="">Somewhere else \u2014 or rather not say</option>` was JSX text
and printed the backslash.

Four sites, all from `7aa0e66` ("Say what the reader's own state does to the
benefit"), all shipped for fourteen commits:

- the state selector's first option — the default one, so this was on screen on
  first paint for every reader who never opened the menu;
- the field note under the selector ("leaves the benefit alone \u2014 with or
  without an income tax of its own \u2014");
- the state footnote's federal-only warning, twice.

Two more sat in a JSX comment, where they were harmless; the working tree had
fixed those too and they are kept, since the comment is read by whoever edits the
line next.

Why nothing caught it: `npx tsc -b` and `eslint` both see well-formed JSX text, and
the two tests that touch these strings assert substrings that stop before the dash
(`App.test.tsx:3160`, `:3168`). No test on this page asserts a whole sentence.

### The guard: `src/prose.test.ts`

Rendering and reading `textContent` would have covered the two unconditional
sites and missed the two behind `statesTaxing.length > 0`. So the scan is at the
source instead: `typescript` is already a devDependency for `tsc -b`, so the test
parses every `.tsx` under `src/` with `ts.createSourceFile(..., ScriptKind.TSX)`
and walks for two node kinds.

- `ts.isJsxText` — 1,330 of them.
- `ts.isJsxAttribute` whose initializer is a `StringLiteral` — 301. A `{'...'}`
  expression attribute is a real string literal and is skipped on purpose.

The claim is wider than one escape: **no backslash at all** in either position.
`\n`, `\t` and `\'` fail there for the same reason `\u2014` does, and a backslash
that genuinely belongs on the page is spelled `{'\\'}`. Both `it`s open with a
count assertion (`>100`, `>50`) so an extractor that walks nothing cannot pass
vacuously — the same guard `styles.test.tsx` puts on its selector list.

The one escape this cannot see is `{'\u2014'}`, which is the spelling that works.

### Verified

- `npx tsc -b`, `npx eslint src --max-warnings 0`, `npx vitest run`
  (11 files, 675 tests — +2, both new).
- Both new invariants falsified once each: putting `\u2014` back into the option's
  JSX text fails the first with `src/App.tsx:2081: Somewhere else \u2014 or rather
  not say`; adding `title="a \u2014 b"` to the same element fails the second.
- The scanner run against `46a7448` itself lists exactly the four sites and no
  others, which is what says the working tree's fix was complete rather than
  partial.
- `npm run build`, then read the text back out of the minified bundle:
  `Somewhere else — or rather not say`, and zero occurrences of `u2014` anywhere
  in `dist` (every legitimate string-literal escape resolves at build time, so a
  surviving one would be a literal).

### Two process notes

`46a7448` wrote its whole write-up into the commit message and never touched
`ralph/ralph.log`, so the log's last entry is step 2 of the visual overhaul while
the repo is on step 3. Nothing is lost — the message is thorough — but a reader
who greps the log for the controls pass will not find it.

And the tree has now opened dirty two runs running. The existing Discovered Work
bullet asks where a `git status --porcelain` guard belongs; a second occurrence
inside a day is the argument for answering it. Recovery was cheap both times, but
only because the diff happened to be legible. Noted both above.

### Left standing

The visual overhaul's step 4 ("Re-shape the shell") is still the topmost unchecked
bullet and is untouched by this run.

## 2026-08-21 — Task: Re-shape the shell (visual overhaul, step 4 of 6)

### The decision the bullet asked for

Both halves of it, yes.

*Step 1's inputs become the column.* Everything that describes the **return** —
the year, the filing status, the ages, the benefit, the home state, and the two
advanced amounts — is now a persistent 18rem column that stays on screen for the
whole walk. The reading column beside it caps at 44rem and holds the charts,
their sliders, the explainers and the close.

Step 1 keeps its number, its heading, its `#step-benefit` id, its scenario recap
and its next-step box. It is still step 1 of 4 and the nav still points at it;
what changed is where it stands, not what it is. That was the cheap decision:
renumbering to three steps would have rewritten every kicker, every next-step
box and about a dozen tests, in exchange for nothing a reader would notice.

*The step nav becomes a header.* It sits at the head of the reading column and
sticks there, opaque in `--surface`, at `z-index: 20`. So the configuration is
always in reach on the left and where-you-are is always legible on the right.

*What did not move, and why.* The sliders in steps 2, 3 and 4 stay in the flow.
They are not only inputs — each is the cursor on the chart directly above it,
and the shape every step has kept since the flow replaced the tab strip is
chart, then the one control that moves you along it. Pulling them into the
column would take each cursor away from the plot it marks.

### What was actually wrong

`#root { max-width: 900px }` was one number doing two jobs: it was the reading
measure *and* the whole width of the page. So there was nowhere to put a second
column, and every input ended up stacked at the top of step 1 — which is why a
reader looking at step 3's chart could not see the benefit being priced under
it without scrolling a screen and a half back and losing their place.

Now `--measure: 44rem`, `--column: 18rem` and `--gutter: 2.5rem` are `:root`
tokens and `#root` is `calc()`'d from them, so the page's width is a consequence
of its columns rather than a third number kept in step by hand.

### The three things the reshape broke, found by rendering it

- **The nav wrapped to two rows.** It was sized for 900px and the reading column
  runs 640px on a large window, so four items no longer fitted. Measured at 97px
  tall — taller than the 68px `scroll-margin-top` meant to clear it, so a step
  arrived at from the nav landed with its heading under the header. Tightened to
  .875rem in .6875rem of padding with a 1.25rem number: 49px, one row, clears
  with 19px to spare. A header whose height changes with the window is a header
  nothing below it can be positioned against.
- **The hint bubble was wider than the column it lives in.** `.hint-bubble` caps
  at `min(26rem, calc(100vw - 3rem))`, and 26rem is wider than 18rem — inside a
  scroll container that is cut-off words and a horizontal scrollbar. `100%` is
  now a third term in the `min()`, which reads as "and never wider than what it
  is anchored in" and needs no new selector.
- **The close stopped being the footer's sibling.** `nextElementSibling` was how
  one test read "ends the page, before the disclaimer", and the footer now spans
  both columns. Re-read in two hops instead: the close is last in the reading
  column, the reading column is last in the shell, the disclaimer follows the
  shell.

### The invariants

`the shell` in `styles.test.tsx`, three of them, in the shape the other passes
use — a closed list that fails when a number is typed into whichever rule is
being edited:

- **sets every column width from the measures it names, never from a number** —
  no `width`/`max-width` in px anywhere in the screen half. `min-width` is
  excluded (a 320px floor is a statement about the smallest window, not a
  column) and so is any rule containing `clip-path` (`.live-reading` is a box
  shrunk to a pixel to keep a live region in the a11y tree; its 1px is a way of
  hiding, not a width).
- **spends every measure it declares** — a token declared and never referenced
  fails, the way `--orange` would have.
- **unpins both columns where the grid collapses to one** — every
  `position: sticky` in the screen half must be taken back to `static` inside
  `@media (max-width: 992px)`. On a phone a pinned configuration panel *is* the
  screen and a two-row pinned nav takes a third of what is left, and forgetting
  one is a bug nobody sees on the machine they wrote it on.

The collapse block is last in the screen half deliberately: a media query adds
no specificity, so a rule it overrides has to be above it in the file.

### Verified

- `npx tsc -b`, `npx eslint src --max-warnings 0`, `npx vitest run`
  (11 files, 678 tests — +3, all three new), `npm run build`, and the tokens
  read back out of the minified `dist` CSS (`900px`: 0 occurrences).
- All three new invariants falsified once each: `max-width: 900px` on `.flow`
  fails the first; inlining `2.5rem` for both uses of `--gutter` fails the
  second; deleting `.step-nav { position: static }` from the collapse block
  fails the third with `expected [ '.step-nav' ] to deeply equal []`.
- Rendered rather than reasoned about. Built, `vite preview`, drove Chromium
  through `playwright-core` at 1440x900, 1100x900, 900x900 and 420x800.
  Two columns of 288px + 640px above 992px, one column below it; document
  `scrollWidth === clientWidth` at every one of the four, so nothing overflows
  sideways. Scrolled to step 3 on a wide window: the column is still at y=0 and
  the benefit slider is still inside the viewport, which is the bullet's own
  complaint, answered. Clicked all three nav items and compared the nav's bottom
  edge against the landed step's kicker: 49 vs 68/109/109, clear every time.
  Hovered the hint: 276px inside a 276px content box. Under
  `emulateMedia({ media: 'print' })` the shell is `block`, the column is
  `static`/`visible`/`max-height: none` and the nav is `display: none`. Zero
  page errors at any size. Screenshotted the hero, step 3 mid-walk, and the
  phone collapse.
- Note for a future iteration: the Chromium/`playwright-core` version skew from
  the last run is unchanged — cached build 1228, `playwright-core` 1.62.1 wants
  1234 — so `chromium.launch()` still needs `executablePath` at
  `~/Library/Caches/ms-playwright/chromium_headless_shell-1228/chrome-headless-shell-mac-arm64/chrome-headless-shell`,
  and the driving script still has to live in the project root for the bare
  `playwright-core` import to resolve.

### Left standing, and why

The `.step-config .next-step` box — "Next · Step 2 of 4" — is now a footer to a
column rather than the end of a section, so it is rendered smaller there and
back at full size in the collapse. Keeping it was the call: on a narrow window
it is still the only way forward for a reader who never looks at the nav, and on
a wide one it is a harmless hand-off from the column to the flow.

The filing-status strip wraps to three rows in 18rem, and the state footnote is
a long paragraph in a narrow measure. Both are legible and both are about to be
overtaken — two later backlog bullets remove the year selector and every
reference to states outright — so neither was worth a rule here.

The tree opened clean this run, which is the first time in three.

## 2026-08-21 — Task: Re-theme the four charts (visual overhaul, step 5 of 6)

There are three, not four. The bullet was written while the Over Time tab still
rendered one; 5fe9854 removed it. Left the bullet's wording alone and noted the
count here instead.

### What the charts were drawn with

Every number in the three plots had been typed into whichever chart was being
edited, so none of them agreed with the page around it:

- **11px labels under 15px ticks.** The tick values inherited body copy, which
  the type pass had left at .9375rem; the cliff names, the marker and the
  ceiling were all at a hard-coded `11`. So the axis shouted and the things it
  was annotating whispered, and neither size was on the page's own scale.
- **A 1px IRMAA cliff beside a 2px marker.** Nothing said why. Five cliffs took
  recharts' default width and the two lines added later did not.
- **A wash whose real alpha was 0.3.** The gradient's top stop was `0.5`, and
  recharts multiplies an `<Area>`'s fill by its own default `fillOpacity` of
  0.6 — so the number in the source was one nobody could read off the screen.
- **A dashed grid, drawn both ways.** `strokeDasharray="3 3"` in both
  directions, across a plot whose *meaningful* lines are dashed verticals: on
  step 2 the mesh ran straight through five dashed cliffs, a dashed subsidy
  cliff and a dashed marker.
- **An axis line the same colour as its own numbers.** recharts defaults a tick
  label's `fill` to the axis's `stroke`, and both were `--ink-muted` — chrome
  painted at content's weight, in a register whose whole premise is the
  opposite.
- **A 70px y-axis gutter**, sized for 15px ticks, written once in the TSX
  (`width={70}`) and three more times in CSS as `padding-left: 70px`, with
  nothing holding the four together.
- **recharts' own `#ccc` and `#fff`** on the hover cursor and the active dot:
  two colours this page does not declare, and the brightest marks on the plot.

### What it is drawn with now

`CHART` in `palette.ts`, beside `PALETTE` and for exactly the same reason: an
SVG `stroke-width` is an attribute and an attribute cannot hold a `var(--…)`,
so the page's scales have to be written a second time for a chart to spend
them. Five entries — `label: 13`, `line: 3`, `rule: 2`, `hairline: 1`,
`fill: 0.2`, `axis: 44`.

Three tiers of chrome, each spending a token `:root` already declares: the
frame is `--edge-strong`, the mesh behind it is `--edge`, the words are
`--ink-muted`. The grid is solid and horizontal only, so a vertical line on
this page means something again. `tickLine` is off. All six axes take one
`AXIS_PROPS` object rather than six copies of the same four props.

Each `<Area>` now sets `fillOpacity={1}` beside its gradient, so `CHART.fill`
is the alpha rather than a factor of it.

The tooltip moved out of `App.tsx` and into the stylesheet. It had been an
inline `TOOLTIP_STYLE` plus a `fontSize` on nearly every row — 15px for the
priced figures, 13px for the notes beside them, 14px for the advice at the
foot — which is a stack of decisions made one row at a time rather than a
size. An inline style is also a rule no scale can see: the `8px` corner and
all three sizes sat outside the lists that hold the rest of the page closed.
As `.chart-tooltip` it takes `var(--radius-lg)`, `var(--surface-raised)`, a
hairline `var(--edge)` and one `0.8125rem` for everything inside it. It also
gets `--shadow-float`, which the ground pass reserved for "the thing that
genuinely floats over the page rather than sitting on it" and then gave to
nothing: a tooltip that follows the pointer across a curve is exactly that.

What stays inline in `App.tsx` is colour, and only the colours that carry
meaning — a row is emerald because it is a gain, violet because it is the
surtax — which is `PALETTE` being spent the same way the SVG spends it.

`--chart-axis: 44px` is the one number a stylesheet and an SVG attribute both
have to know. 13px inside the plot is `0.8125rem` under it, so `.chart-axis-label`
came down from body copy to join `.chart-key` and `.chart-caption`: the chart
and its notes now read at one size.

### The guards

- **`the chart register`** (App.chart.test.tsx, 5 new). Reads the numbers back
  off the rendered SVG rather than off the source, because the surface is the
  only place a scale is either kept or lost: every `font-size` in the three
  plots is `CHART.label`; every `stroke-width` is one of the three steps; the
  curve, the reference line and the grid each take the one that names them;
  the grid rules one way only and the sole dasharrays left are the cliffs' and
  the marker's; and every wash is `CHART.fill`.
- **`the chart metrics`** (styles.test.tsx, 2 new). Pairs the two entries that
  have a CSS counterpart — the gutter against `--chart-axis`, and
  `CHART.label / 16` against the step `.chart-axis-label`, `.chart-key` and
  `.chart-caption` are all set in. The three stroke widths have no CSS side,
  and the register test above is what holds those.

