## 2026-08-21 — Task: Re-derive the close, the explainers and the print sheet from the new ground

The tree opened dirty again, and this time with most of this bullet already
in it: `src/index.css` and `src/styles.test.tsx` carried an unfinished pass
and `shot.mjs`/`probe.mjs` were the scratch drivers written for it. So this
run is the second half — read what was there, finish it, verify all of it,
commit it as one thing. Noted in Discovered Work as a repeat, because it is
now three runs in a row and the write-up for the middle one says the same.

### The close and the boxes

`.answer` and `.next-step` were an accent wash inside an accent border at 25%
alpha. A tint is the old card's idiom — it was how a panel separated itself
from a slate gradient — and the flat near-black ground has no other example
of one. Both come down to `--surface-raised` and a hairline `--edge`, which
is what every other box on the page became in the ground pass. The accent
moves to the kicker and the arrow, which is where FI Calc spends it: on the
words that say what a box is for, not on the box.

They came off the tint together on purpose. The whole reason the close takes
the next-step box's treatment is that they are the same object at two scales
— "this is the end of a section, and here is where it goes".

`.answer-figure`'s left rule was `rgba(--accent-rgb, 0.35)` where
`.scenario-recap`, `.slider-advice` and `.conversion-advice` are all 0.5. One
number, one place, and now guarded.

### The explainers

2.5rem of margin over 2rem of padding around a rule nobody could see. That
spacing was chosen for a 10%-alpha slate border that came to almost nothing
over the old ground; `--edge` is a real hairline, and at that spacing a step
with five collapsed explainers read as five sections rather than one list.
Both come to 1.25rem, so the rule sits close to the heading it divides. The
old air is spent once, on `:first-of-type`, which is the one that has to
clear the chart and the slider above it. Every explainer on this page is a
`<details>` and nothing else in a step is, so `:first-of-type` finds the head
of the group wherever the group starts.

`.step + .step` was `--edge` — the same hairline as an explainer boundary, so
a step break and a disclosure break were painted identically and the page had
two levels of structure at one weight. It takes `--edge-strong`, which until
now was spent only on controls.

### The print sheet

It was forty-odd selectors each restating a screen rule in a slate literal,
because when it was written the colours were spelled where they were used and
there was nothing else to re-point. The ground pass put them all in `:root`.
So the paper half is now a `:root` of its own — five tiers of ink, two edges,
one accent, the eight data hues — and every rule in the screen half paints
itself. The block went from 256 lines to 241 while covering strictly more.

Three things that do not read off the rules, and are written above them:

- **The ink ramp keeps its five tiers and floors them higher.** 15.5:1 down to
  4.7:1 on screen; 21:1 down to 7.3:1 here. The two media fail in opposite
  directions — ink too light on a screen glares, ink too light on paper is
  not there, and it gets lighter again through a photocopier.
- **`-bright` means further from the ground, so on paper it means darker.**
  The name is about contrast, not luminance, and this is the one place the
  distinction shows.
- **The data hues keep their identities.** Amber is still the marker, rose is
  still an IRMAA cliff. Each is the 700/800 step of the same hue and each
  clears 4.5:1 on white, because they carry the text in the keys.

What stays below the tokens is the part that is about paper rather than about
colour: the sheet's shape, what a sheet cannot be, the charts, the
pagination. Two controls needed keeping — the chosen segmented option and the
checkbox tick both state their answer with a fill, and a fill is the one thing
a printer may drop, so both restate it in ink. An unticked box on a printed
return is not a cosmetic fault, it is the wrong answer to "are you 65".

### The charts on paper (the second bullet, struck with this one)

`height: auto !important` on `.chart-container` and both recharts divs was
there to scale a screen-sized SVG down to the sheet via its `viewBox`.
`ResponsiveContainer` measured the change, got zero, and unmounted all three
plots. Thirty tick values on screen, none on paper, and what printed was an
axis label, a key and a caption with nothing between them.

It wants a height chosen for paper instead, and nothing else: recharts does
re-measure for the printer, so the plots re-render at the width of the sheet
and there is no screen-sized SVG left to scale. `height: 300px` — 3.1in,
which leaves a chart and everything under it on one sheet. Confirmed on a
built page: 10 ticks per plot at both 1440px and 701px (7.3in of Letter),
SVG width tracking the sheet, no overflow at either.

### The guards

- **`the print stylesheet`** (2 new). The same line the screen half is held
  to — a colour written anywhere but `:root` fails — plus the one that is
  specific to having two grounds: every colour token the screen declares must
  be *declared* on paper too. That second one is the new failure mode this
  design trades into. The old sheet went stale by a screen rule changing and
  its print twin not; this one goes stale by a token being added to the screen
  ground and not to the paper one, which is silent in exactly the same way,
  because a custom property paper never re-points still resolves — to the
  near-black value. `--surface-control` unanswered is a #31333f block on white
  paper. Only colour tokens are checked: `--measure` and the three corners
  mean the same on either ground.
- **`the print sheet's charts`** (2 new). The print sheet may resize a chart
  and may not take its height away. Nothing reported the bug and nothing
  could — recharts renders nothing under jsdom either, so no render-based test
  can tell a chart missing from paper apart from one missing from the test.
  What is checkable is the rule that did it.
- **`the margin rules`** (1 new). This page sets something apart in the margin
  more often than it does anything else — the recap, the advice under each
  slider, the state footnote, the eight closing figures — and they had drifted
  into two alphas. A margin rule is 2px and either `--edge` or a data token at
  exactly half alpha; a note box is 3px solid down the side of something with
  a fill of its own, and is allowed for explicitly rather than by omission,
  because the difference between a rule beside prose and a box is the point.

### Verified

- `npx tsc -b`, `npx eslint src --max-warnings 0`, `npx vitest run`
  (11 files, 690 tests — +5, all five new), `npm run build`.
- All five falsified once each: a `color: #333` inside `@media print` fails
  the one-place claim; deleting `--surface-control` from the paper ground
  fails the answers-for-every-token claim; `height: auto` back on
  `.chart-container` fails both chart claims; and `.answer-figure` at either
  0.35 alpha or 3px fails the margin-rule claim. `src/index.css` diffed clean
  against its backup afterwards.
- Rendered rather than reasoned about. Built, `vite preview`, drove Chromium
  through `playwright-core`. On screen: the close and the next-step box both
  `rgb(32,33,36)` on `rgb(52,57,64)`, every margin rule 2px at 0.5 (or
