ask for: `git status --porcelain` alone says "dirty", which is the same word for
a finished pass and a broken one. The pair that distinguishes them is a status
check *and* a test run, first thing.

### What was already done, and kept

`PAGE_TAX_YEAR` is a constant in `tax.ts`, deliberately not `defaultTaxYear()`.
That function follows the wall calendar, so a page built on it re-prices itself
the January after a Rev. Proc. lands and a link sent in December means something
else in January. A constant moves when someone edits the line, which is the same
moment they check the figures behind it. Everything below `TAX_YEARS` stays
parameterized by year — the engine prices any year on file, `tax.test.ts`
exercises both, the shelved projection module walks across them. This is the
render layer's choice, not the calculation's.

The year also came out of the link. It was the one key written
unconditionally, because its default moved on its own; with a constant there is
nothing left to pin, so an untouched page now encodes to the empty string and
`scenarioUrl` stops writing a bare `?`. Old links still open: `?year=2025` is
read past in silence, no note, because a reader arriving on one has nothing to
be told and no control to be pointed at. Same reading `?state=VT` got last run.

### What this run added

**Six failing tests, fixed.** Five were 2025 dollars the earlier pass missed
when it re-pointed the rest of `App.test.tsx` at 2026: the tooltip's headroom
figure (recomputed from scratch — provisional $32,426, $3,713 of benefit
taxable, MAGI $23,713 against a $109,000 cliff, so $85,287, not the $84,603 that
had been written in), the ceiling list's IRMAA line, two closing figures and one
stale `toBeGreaterThan` anchor. The sixth was `Tax Year` still listed among the
fieldset legends that must not be behind a disclosure.

**`App.chart.test.tsx`'s 2025 test.** It clicked to 2025, checked the pink line
vanished, and clicked back. The page cannot be sent to 2025 any more, so the
test is replaced by the premise the other four in that describe rest on — the
line is drawn because the year the page prices has a cliff, asserted through
`FPL_YEAR_PARAMS[PAGE_TAX_YEAR].cliff`, not because the chart always draws one.
The year-by-year half was already `tax.test.ts`'s: `ptcCliff({ year: 2025 })`
pinned at null, `fpl400` pinned out of the ceiling list. That file's header also
still claimed the app opens on `defaultTaxYear()`; it now pins to
`PAGE_TAX_YEAR`, which closes Backlog 4's bullet about it asserting on figures
it does not name.

**An engine-level test for the constant.** `tax.test.ts` pins that
`PAGE_TAX_YEAR` is in `TAX_YEARS` and has published params. Not a duplicate of
