## 2026-08-21 — Task: Move Medicare's IRMAA cliffs to a tooltip; add a settings button on the torpedo chart with checkboxes for the IRMAA and 400% FPL breakpoints, default off.

### What the bullet was asking for, and how I read it

Two halves. The second is unambiguous: a settings control on step 2's chart,
opening checkboxes that decide whether the IRMAA lines and the 400% FPL line
are drawn, both starting off. The first — "move the IRMAA cliffs to be a tool
tip" — had to be read against what was actually on the page, which was two
things and not one. The *lines* were on the chart; underneath them sat two
paragraphs of `.chart-key`, one per cliff, carrying what a cliff is, what each
one costs and where it falls. Meanwhile the hover tooltip had been pricing
IRMAA per point since the lines landed — tier, annual surcharge, headroom to
the next cliff and what the next step costs.

So "move to a tooltip" is: the always-on prose goes, and the per-point figure
in the tooltip becomes where a reader meets the cliff. That reading also makes
the two halves one change rather than two: with the lines off by default, a
paragraph of key explaining a dash that is not drawn is worse than nothing.

### What is on the page now

**A `Lines` button above the plot, right-aligned.** Not a gear: this page draws
its one affordance out of two borders (`details.explainer summary::after`) and
owns no icon asset, so the button takes the same chevron and rotates it the
same way. It carries a count of what is actually *drawn* — `Lines (4)` — which
is not the number of ticked boxes: tick IRMAA on a joint return whose first
threshold is past the right edge and it stays `Lines`. That difference is the
whole reason the notes inside the panel exist.

**A panel with two checkboxes, both off.** Each carries the dashed swatch in
its own hue, so the panel is the key as well as the switch. Under each one, one
sentence: what ticking it will draw and where. IRMAA gets `cliffPriceList`
verbatim — the same "IRMAA 1 at $87,876 costs $1,148/yr; …" the deleted key
carried — or, when none fits, where the nearest one is. The 400% switch is only
offered to a return that could still claim the credit (`preMedicare`) in a year
that has a cliff, which is the pair of conditions its explainer already used.

**The tooltip carries both cliffs.** IRMAA's four rows were already there. The
400% line is new: `ptcFor(acaMagi(scenario), scenario)` at the hovered point,
saying either the household income left before the credit goes or that it has
gone. Worth having as a third row rather than a rescaling of Medicare's,
because 36B counts the *whole* benefit where Medicare counts only the share the
torpedo dragged in — the same reason the two lines never move together.

**Both `.chart-key` paragraphs deleted.** Nothing in them was lost: the prices
are in the panel, the per-point standing is in the tooltip, and everything else
("a cliff, not a phase-in", the two-year lag, what the household pays under the
400% line) was already in the two disclosures below, which are unchanged apart
from their headings. Those headings said "the red dashed lines" and "the pink
dashed line" and now cannot promise that, so each names the setting instead —
which is also the only discoverability the panel gets.

Escape closes the panel and returns focus to the button; a mousedown outside it
closes it too. The outside-click ref wraps button *and* panel, or the listener
would close it a moment before the button's own handler reopened it.

