## Give the gain an average rather than a next dollar

Backlog 5, "in cap gains section, make the graph the effective rate for
capital gains, not marginal rate." Step 3's chart plotted `marginalRate` — the
cost of the next dollar of gain — as a `stepAfter` staircase. It now plots
`effectiveRate`, drawn `monotone`, and the staircase is gone.

### The definition

`effectiveRate` on `LTCGRatePoint` is the share of the gain itself that
federal tax takes: `totalFederalTax(here)` less what the same return would owe
with that gain never realized, over the gain. Zero at $0 of gain, where there
is nothing to average.

The counterfactual was the one real decision. Three were on the table and two
are wrong for this: "the gain taxed as ordinary income instead" gives a
negative number (a saving, which the readout already reports as `mixSaving`),
and "the gain's own preferential-schedule tax" is a flat zero for any reader
whose taxable income never clears the 0% ceiling — true and useless. What went
in is "the gain was never realized", which reads the same way in both sweep
modes: `{ ...here, ltcg: 0 }` with nothing put in its place. On the additive
sweep that makes the curve the integral of the marginal one over its own
width; on step 3's `gainsWithinIncome` sweep it prices the gain against the
smaller all-ordinary return the reader would have had without it, so the
torpedo the gain drags in lands on the gain, which is the section's subject.

### The shape does not match the bullet

The bullet predicted "graph starts increasing after the 15% threshold". It
does not, and cannot on this axis. That prediction is the additive picture —
gain stacked on a fixed income, flat at nothing under the 0% ceiling and
climbing toward 15% past it, which is exactly what the curve does with
`gainsWithinIncome` off. Step 3 sweeps it on: total income is held still and
only the split moves, so a gain that sits above the 0% ceiling at the left
edge reaches *down* into it as it grows and the average falls. Measured on the
built page: $150,000 single is 15% flat to about $40,000 of gain and then
declines to 8.1% at the right edge; the page as it opens ($30,000 of income,
$24,852 of benefit) rises to 10.2%, holds, and falls to nothing.

Both curves are true about the same return; they answer different questions,
and choosing between them is choosing the axis rather than the series. Logged
under Discovered work rather than decided here — and the backlog bullet three
below asks to shelve this whole section anyway.

### What else moved

- The tooltip leads with `Effective Rate on the Gain` and keeps
  `Next Dollar of Gain` under it. The second line is not decoration: the hill
  and valley advice below it is cut on the marginal rate, and without it that
  advice has nothing on the card to hang off.
- That advice was reworded. `segmentCurve` still cuts on `marginalRate`, which
  is no longer drawn, so "Consider avoiding this tax hill" pointed at a feature
  the reader cannot see. It now says "The next dollar of gain is dearest
  through here — consider staying under $X or over $Y". Step 2's tooltip keeps
  the old wording; it still plots the marginal rate.
- The slider readout forks at $0. "Federal tax takes 0% of the gain itself" is
  true, says nothing, and is where the page opens — so with no gain set it
  leads with what the first dollar taken as gain would cost instead. The live
  region takes the same fork.
- The "Why the two effects stack" explainer's third paragraph was about the
  height of the curve being the next dollar. Rewritten to the average, with the
  0%-ceiling arithmetic spelled out and a pointer to the hover for the other
  rate.
- `ltcgMarginalRateCurve` → `ltcgRateCurve`, `LTCGMarginalRatePoint` →
  `LTCGRatePoint`. The type carries two rates now and the old names named one.

### Tests

680 → 689. Five in `tax.test.ts` under a new `effectiveRate` describe: zero at
the origin, flat at nothing under the 0% ceiling and strictly climbing past it
without reaching 15% (the additive sweep), the counterfactual pinned against
`totalFederalTax` directly, past 15% where the torpedo lands on the gain, and
the same counterfactual on a within-income sweep. Two in `App.chart.test.tsx`:
the curve starts on the zero line (flipping the `dataKey` back to
`marginalRate` fails it — 64 against an axis at 370), and it emits cubic
segments where step 2's staircase emits none. Two in `App.test.tsx` for the
tooltip's two rate lines and the readout's two figures.

One assertion I wrote was wrong and the run caught it: I had the whole
return's effective rate as the larger of the two figures. At $20,000 of
ordinary income and a $100,000 gain it is the smaller — 7.34% against the
gain's own 8.38% — because the standard deduction shelters nearly all of the
ordinary half, so the gain carries the bill and the other income only dilutes
the denominator. The test now says that, with the $500 of ordinary tax pinned
beside it.

### Verified

- `npx tsc -b`, `npx eslint src --max-warnings 0`, `npx vitest run`
  (11 files, 689 tests), `npm run build`.
- Rendered rather than reasoned about. Built, `vite preview` on 4185, drove
