## 2026-08-21 — Task: if married filing jointly, social security slider should have different maximum and average than single.

`ssBenefit` is line 6a of the return, and on a joint return line 6a is two
people's benefits added together. The slider was setting one worker's either
way: `maxAnnualSSBenefit(year)` and `avgAnnualSSBenefit(year)` took a year and
nothing else, so a couple who both collect were being asked to describe
themselves with a ceiling half their size and a marker that named the wrong
average.

### The two figures, and why only one of them doubles

`TaxYearParams` gains `maxAnnualCoupleSSBenefit` and
`avgAnnualCoupleSSBenefit`:

- 2025: $122,592 max, $37,068 avg ($3,089/mo)
- 2026: $124,344 max, $38,496 avg ($3,208/mo)

The maximum is exactly two of the worker maximum — two records, 35 years at the
taxable maximum each, each claimed at 70 — and a test walks `TAX_YEARS`
asserting that doubling, so a year added to the table cannot get one end right
and the other wrong.

The average is not two of the worker average and is the interesting half. SSA
publishes "aged couple, both receiving benefits" separately, and at $3,208/mo
against $2,071/mo it is 1.55x rather than 2x, because in a large share of those
couples the second benefit is a spousal one — half the higher earner's PIA, not
a second record. That is why it has to be a table entry rather than an
arithmetic derivation, and the same test pins it strictly between one worker
average and two.

Both accessors now take `(year, filingStatus = 'single')` — the argument order
`filingParams` already uses. The default keeps every call site that predates
this meaning what it meant.

### What the page does with them

- Slider `max`, the middle range marker and the right-hand one all read the
  status figures. Joint markers say "2026 couple avg" / "2026 couple max", and
  the control's own label becomes "Annual Social Security Benefit (both
  spouses)".
- A field note appears under the joint slider only, saying what the two ends
  are and why the average is not the single one doubled.
- `changeFilingStatus` re-seats the benefit. The re-cap is the same one the
  charitable gift already gets — coming back from `mfj` can leave a figure
  standing past a right edge that just halved. The second rule is new: a reader
  sitting exactly on one status's average has accepted the marker rather than
  chosen a number, so the marker takes them with it, and switching back puts
  them exactly where they started. Anywhere else on the slider is theirs and
  stays put.

### The link

`decodeScenario` clamped `ss` against the worker maximum regardless of the
`filing` key in the same link, which would have silently halved a joint
benefit on a link the page itself wrote. Both the fallback and the max now read
the decoded status (the ordering was already right — `filingStatus` is parsed
first, and `qcd` already did this). The note names the couple bound when it
cuts one back. `encodeScenario` measures the benefit against the average for
the status it is writing, so `?filing=mfj` alone is a complete link to a joint
return at the couple average.

### What moved downstream, and why it is right

Seven existing tests changed their numbers because a joint return now carries
$38,496 instead of $24,852, which puts 85% of $13,644 more into MAGI and pulls
every MAGI-denominated feature $11,598 left in other-income terms:

- The joint first IRMAA cliff moves from $196,876 to $185,278 of other income,
  and on the senior-deduction chart (axis $250,000) the second cliff at
  $241,278 now fits where $252,876 did not. Two chart tests and two note tests.
- A joint conversion to the top of the 22% bracket fits $180,878 rather than
  $192,475 — same ceiling, less room under it.
- The link round-trip test now reads $158,496 of total income, and asserts the
  link carries no `ss=` key, which is the new encode rule stated as a fact
  about the artefact.
- `scenarioUrl.test.ts`'s `moved()` helper seeds the benefit from the status it
  is given, because a joint scenario at the single average is one the page can
  no longer produce.

### Tests

689 -> 697. Four in `App.test.tsx` under a new describe: the doubled ceiling
and moved markers, the average carrying a reader across and back, a set figure
staying put and being re-capped on the way back, and every other status still
setting one person's. Two in `tax.test.ts` for the accessors across all years
and for the ceiling stretching further than the average does, plus three
assertions folded into the well-formed-schedule sweep. Two in
`scenarioUrl.test.ts`: a bare `filing=mfj` reading as the couple average, and a
joint link carrying $124,344 whole where a single one is cut to $62,172.

### One CSS change

`.slider-range-labels` gets `gap: 0.5rem`. Three `space-between` markers fit on
one line in the 18rem configuration column at "$24,852 (2026 avg)" but not at
"$38,496 (2026 couple avg)" — "$0" was butting straight against the next
marker. Measured in the browser, not reasoned about.

