## 2026-08-21 — Task: remove the sections for capital gains and roth conversion from the page to keep it focused on tax torpedo.

The tree opened dirty holding almost all of this task and one failing test. Six
files, ~2,500 lines deleted, PLAN.md unmarked, ralph.log unwritten — an
abandoned run, and the fifth in a row to leave one. The first two commands of
this run (`git status --porcelain`, `npx vitest run`) said within ten seconds
which of the two it was: finished work with a loose end, not a half-edit worth
reverting. So this run finished it rather than re-doing it, and the write-up
below separates what arrived in the tree from what this pass added.

### What was already in the tree

Steps 3 and 4 gone from `STEPS`, from the nav, from the charts and from the
close; `plannedLtcg` and `ceilingId` out of `PageScenario`; `ltcg` and
`ceiling` read past rather than honoured on decode; ~1,000 lines of test either
deleted or rewritten to assert the absence. `tsc -b` and `eslint` were both
already clean. The arithmetic was left alone, which is the task's own
instruction: `ltcgRateCurve`, `conversionCeilings`, `sizeConversion` and
`niitFor` are all still exported from `utils/tax.ts` and still under test.

The one decision in there worth restating, because it is the one a future run
would otherwise re-litigate: `ltcg` in an old link is **read past in silence**.
It is the dangerous key of the two — it moved the curve — so honouring it would
set a figure no reader could see, change, or be told about. `ceiling` goes the
same way for the same reason.

### The failing test, which was the whole loose end

`the margin rules` in styles.test.tsx counts left borders in the screen block
and opens with `expect(drawn.length).toBeGreaterThan(5)` — an extractor guard,
so that an empty list cannot pass the real assertion vacuously. Taking
`.conversion-advice` out of index.css left exactly five: two note boxes
(`.link-note`, `.warning-note`) at 3px, three margin rules (`.slider-advice`,
`.scenario-recap`, `.answer-figure`) at 2px. The guard is now `> 3` and the
docstring says six closing figures rather than eight. Nothing about the rule
being guarded changed — one weight, one alpha, still true of all five.

### What this pass added on top

- **README.md.** It was still selling four steps, the eight closing figures,
  and `ltcg`/`ceiling` as live query keys, with `#step-conversion` as the
  worked example of a fragment. Now two steps, six figures, seven keys, and a
  paragraph naming what came off and what stayed exported. The 1411 section
  says plainly that the surtax is priced by the engine and is $0 for anything
  the page can currently set — NII is `max(0, ltcg)` and nothing sets `ltcg`
  any more — rather than leaving a reader to infer a live surtax from prose
  written when there was one.
- **`src/shelf/README.md`.** Two sentences that counted the flow's steps to
  argue why `projection.ts` is not one of them.
- **`palette.ts` and the two `:root` comments.** `--indigo` is now spent by no
  rule and no attribute anywhere; `PALETTE.emerald` and `PALETTE.violet` by no
  chart. Kept, all of them — the colours belong to the arithmetic that stayed,
  and re-picking them when the steps come back would be a worse outcome than an
  unspent token. What changed is that the docstrings and the palette header no
  longer claim a step that is not there. `PALETTE.lime`'s was stale in the
  other direction: it says "the 0% long-term gain band" and its only live use
  is the tooltip's charity line.
- **`.step-prose`.** Newly orphaned by the removal — used once, in step 4 — so
  its two rules went. The other class-shaped strings a grep turns up
  (`.configuration_title`, `.mt-checkbox_checkbox`, `.chart-key-subsidy`) are
  all inside comments, not rules; checked before touching anything.

### Verified

- `npx tsc -b`, `npx eslint src --max-warnings 0`, `npx vitest run` (11 files,
  650 tests), `npm run build`.
- Driven in Chromium against the built page. Two `h2` steps and two nav items;
