## 21 Aug 2026 — Say what the link is before anyone opens it

Task: "A shared link previews as a bare URL." — `index.html` had a title and a
description and nothing else, and the favicon was still the template's
`/vite.svg`.

The guard worked this time in the ordinary way: `git status --porcelain` then
`npx vitest run` opened on a clean tree and 655 green, so the run was a build
rather than a rescue. Seven runs of the dirty-tree streak ended.

### What landed

- **`index.html`** — `og:type`/`site_name`/`title`/`description`/`url`/`image`
  with `image:type`, `:width`, `:height`, `:alt`, plus the four `twitter:*`
  and a `theme-color`. Both icon links and all three URLs are built through
  `%BASE_URL%`, which vite substitutes per build: production gets
  `/congenial-octo-spork/`, the preview workflow's `--base` gets
  `/congenial-octo-spork/preview/`, so the preview's card is its own file
  rather than production's. Verified in both builds.
- **`public/og-cover.png`** — 1200x630, 72 kB. The curve on it is the *real*
  one: `marginalRateCurve` sampled for `defaultScenario()`, not a drawn hump.
- **`public/favicon.svg`** + **`public/apple-touch-icon.png`** (180x180, iOS
  will not take an SVG). `public/vite.svg` deleted.
- **`scripts/og-cover.mjs`** — the generator. Bundles `src/utils/{tax,
  scenarioUrl}` with esbuild's JS API into a temp dir, imports it, lays out
  the SVG in the page's own tokens, and rasterises through playwright-core.
- **`src/meta.test.ts`** — 10 tests, and **`README.md`** documents both.

### Two decisions worth writing down

**The card is not the reader's scenario, and cannot be.** This is static files
on Pages: no server ever sees the query string, so nothing can render a
per-link card. A card that quoted figures would quote the *default's* figures
at everyone who shared a link — worse than saying nothing, because it would be
confidently wrong about someone's return. So the card says what the page is
and the figures stay on the page. That reasoning is in the HTML comment
because the next person to look at it will otherwise try to fix it.

**The picture is generated, not drawn.** A hand-drawn hump would go on
claiming 22.2% long after a bracket moved. Bundling the real curve costs ~40
lines and means re-running the script is the whole maintenance story.

### On the labels — the bug the first render had

First pass pinned the "22.2%" dot to the last sample before the fall-back and
the "back to 12%" dot to the first one after it. Both are correct points and
they are ~6px apart, so the two labels stacked into one unreadable column on
the cliff between them. Both features are *plateaux* — the 22.2% run is every
income at which the next dollar drags 85 cents of benefit with it — so
`middleOfRun` walks out to each run's own ends and labels its midpoint. The
drop then separates them without either label leaving the thing it names.

### Verified

- `tsc -b`, `eslint . --max-warnings 0`, `vite build`, `vitest run`: 12 files,
  **665 tests** (655 + 10).
- **Mutation-checked all four load-bearing assertions**, restoring between
  each: relative `og:image` → 2 fail; `og:image:width` 1200→1600 → 1 fail;
  `href="%BASE_URL%favicon.svg"` → `"/favicon.svg"` → 1 fail; description
