## Cut the bundle where it is reused, not where it is scrolled past

**Task:** "Every build trips Vite's chunk warning" (Backlog 4).

The bullet framed it as a choice between a dynamic `import()` of the chart
subtree and raising `chunkSizeWarningLimit` with an apology. Took neither.
Vite's warning lists three remedies and the middle one — `manualChunks` — is
the right one here, so the build now emits three static chunks instead of one.

### Why not the dynamic import

Two of the bullet's premises had gone stale under Backlog 5. There are no
longer "four charts"; there is one, because the capital-gains and
Roth-conversion steps came off the page. And it is not below the fold: with
step 1's inputs now a persistent left column, the chart is the *top* of the
main column — measured at y=578 in a 1000px viewport, so it is on screen on
arrival.

The decisive fact is the waterfall. A chunk behind `import()` cannot be
requested until React has mounted the component that asks for it, so deferring
would buy a faster first paint of an empty box and pay for it by putting a
round trip in front of the one thing the reader came for. A *static* split
does not have that problem — Vite writes a `modulepreload` for each vendor
chunk into `index.html`, so all three still arrive in parallel off one HTML
parse. Confirmed in the built `dist/index.html` and in the browser: three JS
requests, all 200.

And the cost of deferring would have been real: recharts is imported at the
top of `App.tsx` and used by `hereLine`, `AXIS_PROPS` and the tooltip, so
lazy-loading means extracting a chart subtree out of a 2,745-line file and then
teaching every `render(<App />)` in ~3,400 lines of test to await a Suspense
boundary.

### What the split is actually for

What is wrong with one 576 kB chunk is its granularity, not its size. This
repo ships app-code changes daily and nothing else, and every one of those
deploys was invalidating recharts and React along with it.

Measured the per-package weights first with a throwaway config that gave every
`node_modules` package its own chunk. app 66 kB · react-dom 130 · react +
scheduler 12 · recharts 244 · everything under recharts (redux-toolkit, immer,
es-toolkit, decimal.js-light, victory-vendor and eight d3-\*) 125. That is the
whole 576.

Result — 65.56 / 142.20 / 367.69 kB (21.27 / 45.55 / 107.39 gzipped). Total is
575.45 against 576.00, so the split costs 0.3 kB gzipped and nothing else. No
warning, because all three are genuinely under 500 kB rather than under a limit
that was moved.

### The rule, and the invariant under it

    if (!id.includes('node_modules')) return undefined
    return /node_modules\/(react|react-dom|react-is|scheduler)\//.test(id)
      ? 'react' : 'charts'

"Everything in node_modules that is not React is the chart library" is only
true while recharts is the sole other runtime dependency. That is an
invariant, not an observation, so `src/build.test.ts` asserts
`Object.keys(pkg.dependencies)` is exactly `react, react-dom, recharts`. Add a
fourth and the test fails, which is the moment to decide whether a chunk called
`charts` still describes its contents.

### Verified

- `tsc -b`, `eslint . --max-warnings 0`, `vite build` (no warning),
  `vitest run`: 13 files, **670 tests** (665 + 5).
- **Mutation-checked all four load-bearing assertions**, restoring between
  each: delete the `build` block so the rule is defined but unwired → 1 fail;
  widen the regex to `react[a-z-]*` so it swallows `react-redux` → 1 fail; drop
  the `node_modules` guard so app source gets chunked → 1 fail; add a fourth
  entry to `dependencies` → 1 fail; restored → 5 pass.
- Real Chromium against `vite preview` on the built `dist`: zero page errors,
  zero console errors, zero 4xx; all three chunks 200; 10 axis ticks and 1 area
  curve drawn; dragging `#ordinary-income` to $60,000 moved the "you are here"
  label with it. Cross-chunk init order was the one real risk of `manualChunks`
  and this is what rules it out.

### Two things that had to move

`src/setupTests.ts` now guards its `window.history.replaceState` with a
`typeof window !== 'undefined'`. Importing `vite.config.ts` into a test drags
in `@vitejs/plugin-react` and therefore esbuild, which dies under jsdom
("`new TextEncoder().encode("") instanceof Uint8Array` is incorrectly false" —
jsdom's `TextEncoder` is not Node's). So `build.test.ts` carries
`// @vitest-environment node`, and the shared setup file had to stop assuming a
DOM. First non-jsdom test file in the repo.

`vite.config.ts` now has a named `export function manualChunks`. That is what
lets the test call the rule with fabricated module ids instead of asserting on
the config file's text. `tsconfig.app.json` includes only `src`, and importing
`../vite.config` across that boundary turned out to be fine — `tsc -b` passes.

### Note for the next run

The standing browser note in the previous entry has the *shape* right and the
*paths* wrong, so replacing it: on this machine the cache holds
`chromium-1228` and `chromium_headless_shell-1228`, and the binaries are at
`chrome-headless-shell-mac-arm64/chrome-headless-shell` and
`chrome-mac-arm64/Google Chrome for Testing.app/Contents/MacOS/Google Chrome
for Testing` — neither of which is the `chrome-mac/Chromium.app/...` the old
note said to look for. Don't hardcode the layout: read the cache dir, sort
builds by number descending, then for each platform subdirectory try a list of
known binary relative paths and take the first that `existsSync`. That version
is in the log because it survives both a playwright bump and an arch change.
Otherwise unchanged: `pkill -f "vite preview"` first, serve under
`/congenial-octo-spork/`, write the driver to `/tmp` then `cp` it into the root
for the bare `playwright-core` import and `rm` it in the same command line.

