## 21 Aug 2026 — Say the surtax is asleep, and leave something that wakes up

Task: Backlog 6 bullet 1 — "The 1411 surtax is priced everywhere and can never
be anything but zero."

### The choice among the three ways out

The bullet offered shelf it / correct the comments / give the page a
taxable-interest input. Took the middle one, and the reason is in the tree
rather than in taste:

- **Not the shelf.** `src/shelf/README.md` is explicit that the shelf is for
  *whole modules that lost their section*, and that "nothing outside
  `src/shelf/` imports from `src/shelf/`". 1411 is not a module — it is one
  term of `totalFederalTax`, which `marginalRateCurve`, `ltcgRateCurve`,
  `incomeAxisFeatures` and `conversionCeilings` all call. Moving it means
  deleting `totalFederalTax` from live code and re-pointing five call sites at
  `totalTax`, and then re-threading all of it when gains come back. And
  App.tsx:87 has *already made this decision*: it names `niitFor` alongside
  `ltcgRateCurve`, `conversionCeilings` and `sizeConversion` as the things
  "still in `utils/tax.ts`" waiting for the two steps that came off. Shelving
  one of those four splits a group the page's own comment keeps together.
- **Not a taxable-interest input.** Backlog 5's instruction was "keep it
  focused on tax torpedo". The torpedo lives between $20k and $60k; 1411
  starts at $200k. A new slider that earns its keep for nobody on this page is
