## Backlog 6 · bullet 3 — the search snippet advertised a section that is gone

`<meta name="description">` promised "the Social Security tax torpedo,
capital-gains stacking, IRMAA cliffs and the senior deduction phaseout". The
capital-gains step came off the page in Backlog 5 and `App.test.tsx:567`
already asserts its heading is absent — so the one tag a search engine reads
was selling a section that is not there.

### Why nothing caught it

`the link preview` in `src/meta.test.ts` holds `og:description` and
`twitter:description` against each other. Both were rewritten in the pass that
removed the step, so they agreed, and they agreed with nothing. `name="description"`
was never read by any assertion — not for content, not for length, not at all.
Comparing two copies of the same sentence proves they are copies; it does not
prove either one is true.

### The rewrite

    What the next dollar of retirement income really costs, for your own
    return: the tax torpedo, IRMAA cliffs and the senior deduction phaseout.

140 characters, against the old 219. Three deliberate choices:

- **Ends on the list.** The sections it promises are the last clause, so a
  test can read them back out of the prose instead of a second hand-kept list
  drifting beside it.
- **Every phrase is a substring of a heading the page renders.** Rendered
  headings today: `What is the tax torpedo?`, `How to mitigate the tax
  torpedo`, `Medicare's IRMAA cliffs`, `The senior deduction phaseout
  (2025–2028)`. So `the tax torpedo`, `IRMAA cliffs`, `the senior deduction
  phaseout` all land.
- **Leaves out the 400% poverty-line cliff**, even though it renders on the
  default scenario. Its explainer is behind `preMedicare && subsidyCliff` — a
  description that names it would be a promise the page keeps only for some
  readers, which is the same bug one size smaller.

### The test

New `describe('the search snippet')` in `src/meta.test.ts`. It renders the
actual app (`createElement(App)` — the file is `.ts`, not `.tsx`), collects
every heading by role, parses the topic list out of the description's own
prose, and looks for each topic in a heading. Naming a section that came off
the page fails here; so does taking a section off the page without rewriting
the tag. Plus a 160-character cap, which is roughly where Google truncates —
looser 200 for the share card stays where it was, because chat previews are
wider.

Put in `meta.test.ts` rather than `App.test.tsx` because the fact under test
belongs to `index.html`, and that file's stated job is the surfaces no other
test reads. It already imports `utils/tax` to check the card quotes a rate the
engine still reaches, so reaching into the app for the same class of reason is
in keeping.

### The bug in my own test, caught by mutation-testing it

First draft:

```ts
expect(headings.find((h) => h.includes(topic)) ?? `no heading names "${topic}"`).toContain(topic);
```

The fallback message **contains the topic**, so when the lookup missed,
`toContain` matched the fallback and the assertion passed. Mutation 1 — putting
`capital-gains stacking` back in the description — went green, which is the
only reason I found it. A test that cannot fail is worse than no test: it
reports coverage it does not have. Fallback is now `'no heading on the page
says'`, which shares no words with any topic, and the failure reads
`expected 'no heading on the page says' to contain 'capital-gains stacking'`.

**Mutation-checked three ways**, each restored after:

| mutation | result |
| --- | --- |
| `capital-gains stacking` back in the description | red: `expected 'no heading on the page says' to contain 'capital-gains stacking'` |
| page heading renamed to `Medicare's premium surcharges` | red, the same assertion from the other side: `…to contain 'irmaa cliffs'` |
| description padded to 192 characters | red: `expected 192 to be less than or equal to 160` |

