## Backlog 6 · bullet 1 — the nav and the next-step box came off

`role="toolbar"` with two buttons — *Your benefit*, *The tax torpedo* — pinned
to the head of the reading column, and a full-width box at the foot of step 1
reading **Next · Step 2 of 2 / The tax torpedo** with a blurb and a chevron.
Both are gone. They were sized for a four-section walk; on a two-section page
the box spends a screen's width and four lines of prose moving a reader past a
single heading they can already see.

### What went with them

Removing the two renderers stranded everything that fed them, and the whole
chain came out rather than being left as unread code:

| gone | its only reader was |
| --- | --- |
| `goToStep` | the nav's click path and the box |
| `onStepKeyDown`, `stepNavRef` | the roving-tabindex arrow handling |
| `nextStepBox` | itself |
| `step` / `setStep` state | the nav's `aria-current` and `tabIndex` |
| `stepFromHash` | the initialiser of that state |
| `STEPS[].navLabel` / `.heading` / `.blurb` | the nav, the box, the box |

`STEPS` is now `['benefit', 'torpedo'] as const`. Two things still need it and
nothing else can supply them: `StepId`, which `announceFrom` is keyed to, and
`STEPS.length`, which the two step kickers number themselves out of.

**`stepFromHash` is not a loss.** It never scrolled anything — the browser
does that off the `id` — it only marked the right nav button current so a
reader who followed `#step-torpedo` was not shown a nav insisting they were on
step 1. With no nav there is nothing to mark, and `#step-torpedo` still works
because `<section id>` is what the browser was ever reading.

### The stylesheet

`.step-nav` and its five descendants, `.next-step` and its five, the
`.step-config .next-step` footer sizing, both narrow-window overrides, and two
of the five entries in the print sheet's "what a sheet of paper cannot be"
list. Two knock-on edits that are not deletions:

- **`.step { scroll-margin-top }` 4.25rem → 1rem.** The 4.25 was clearance for
  the sticky nav. Nothing sticks above a step now, but a step is still a scroll
  target, so it keeps breathing room rather than losing the property. The
  narrow-window override that already said 1rem is now redundant and went.
- **The close's comment.** It said `.answer` "takes `.next-step`'s treatment
  exactly … they are the same object at different scales" — a comment pointing
  at a rule that no longer exists. The reasoning it was borrowing (hairline on
  a raised surface, accent on the kicker not the box) is now written out at
  `.answer` itself.

### Two tests that fell over on second-order facts

Neither mentions the nav; both were counting things the nav was one of.

- **`the shell > unpins both columns`** guards itself with
  `expect(pinned.length).toBeGreaterThan(1)` so that finding no `position:
  sticky` cannot pass vacuously. The page had exactly two — `.step-config` and
  `.step-nav`. Now one, so the floor is `toBeGreaterThan(0)` and the name is
  "unpins everything that pins".
- **`the corners > spends every step it declares`** holds the radius list
  closed from both directions. `50%` was on it, and `.step-nav-number`'s
  numbered discs were the only rule in the file that drew a circle. `50%` came
  off the list; the comment now says it is welcome back by being drawn, not by
  being listed.

### The new tests

Replacing eight nav tests (numbering, current marking, focus-into-section,
`aria-controls`, walk-forward, arrow keys with wraparound, roving tabindex,
non-arrow keys) with two that hold the removal:

- **`offers nothing that navigates between the steps`** — no `toolbar`, no
  `navigation`, no `[aria-current]`, and no button whose label matches
  `/step \d+ of/i`. Written as a shape rather than by class name so that
  putting either back under a new name still fails.
- **`still numbers both steps where each one starts`** — the two `.step-kicker`
  texts are exactly `Step 1 of 2` and `Step 2 of 2`. The numbering was the only
  thing the nav actually told anyone, and the kickers say it without asking to
  be clicked.

Three existing tests were rewritten rather than deleted, because each had a
claim underneath the nav it was using as a lever: `keeps every input mounted`
(now asserts mounting directly instead of clicking through the nav to prove
it), `advanced inputs keeps its values` (now works step 2's slider), and the
fragment test, which split into "keeps the fragment through a change" and
"renders normally under a fragment that names no step" — the second being the
claim that a `#step-gains` link is inert rather than an error, which is what it
has to be now that nothing reads the fragment.

### Mutation-checked four ways, each reverted

| mutation | result |
| --- | --- |
| a `role="toolbar"` div with an `aria-current` button | red: `expected <div role="toolbar" …> to be null` |
| `Step 2 of {STEPS.length}` → `Step 2` | red: `expected [ 'Step 1 of 2', 'Step 2' ] to deeply equal […]` |
| a `.next-step` button after the recap | red: `expected <button …> to be null` |
| a dead `.step-nav-item` rule left in the stylesheet | **green — see below** |

### The one guard that did not fire

`the stylesheet > scopes no rule to a nesting the page never renders` is built
on `nestedClassSelectors`, so it only reads selectors with a descendant
combinator in them. A flat `.step-nav-item { … }` left behind after its markup
goes is invisible to it. That is the test's stated scope rather than a
regression, and I left it alone — a flat-class version would flag every rule
for a state the default render does not reach (`.link-note`, `.warning-note`,
the copy button's `Copied`). Noted here rather than in PLAN.md because the fix
is a new test with a real false-positive problem, not a chore.

