## Backlog 6 · bullet 2 — two statuses on the strip, two behind a menu

`FILING_STATUS_OPTIONS` rendered all four statuses as one `.segmented` strip
in the order single / hoh / mfj / mfs. The bullet asks for Single and Married
Filing Jointly as the first two options with head of household and a separate
return behind a dropdown, so the constant split in two — `FILING_STATUS_STRIP`
and `FILING_STATUS_MENU` — and the render puts a `<select>` beside the strip.

### Why a native `<select>` rather than the page's own dropdown

The page already owns one dropdown: `.chart-lines`, a button with
`aria-expanded` over a panel, with an outside-click listener and an Escape
handler behind it. Reusing that shape here would have meant a second copy of
that machinery, a second widget to keep keyboard-correct, and a rename of
`.chart-lines-*` to something both could share. A `<select>` is what a choice
among named values already is, and it costs no ARIA, no listeners and no focus
management — which is the same trade the previous iteration made in the other
direction when it deleted a `role="toolbar"` with roving tabindex.

The one thing it costs is the split value: while the menu is holding the
answer, the radio group has nothing checked. That is legal and ordinary — it
is what any unanswered radio question looks like — and the reader's answer is
not hidden by it, because the menu is showing that answer in words and drawn
in the accent fill a chosen segmented option is drawn in. `.filing-more-chosen`
is that fill, and the alternative shapes all had a worse version of the same
problem: a `<details>` or a custom panel holding the extra two radios hides the
*checked* control when it is closed, which is the failure that actually matters,
and forcing it open whenever a hidden status is chosen gives you a disclosure
that will not close.

### What the control does now

- Strip: `Single`, `Married Filing Jointly`, in that order.
- Menu: a disabled `More statuses…` placeholder, then `Head of Household` and
  `Married Filing Separately`. The placeholder is `disabled` so it cannot be
  chosen back — there is no such status, and the way out of the menu is the
  strip beside it.
- `onChange` looks the event's value up in `FILING_STATUS_MENU` and calls
  `changeFilingStatus` with the found entry rather than casting the string to a
  `FilingStatus`, so nothing but those two can be set from here.
- `menuStatus` (App.tsx:941) is the single derivation both the `value` and the
  chosen class read, so the two cannot disagree.

### Layout, measured rather than assumed

`.filing-choices` is a wrapping flex row. In the 18rem configuration column
there is never room for both — the strip alone measures 245px against the
column's 276px — so they stack there; at 900px and 700px, where the shell has
collapsed to one column and the fieldset has the whole measure, they sit side
by side and the chosen menu reads as a third segmented option. Both checked in
a real Chromium against the built page, and the comment on the rule says which
happens where instead of claiming a row it does not always get.

### The print sheet gained a third control

Its "what a fill was carrying" block held two controls that state their answer
with a background colour. The menu is a third, and the one whose answer is
already in words — but the printer that *does* honour backgrounds would put
this sheet's near-black ink on a mid-blue, so the fill comes off and the answer
takes the accent border and ink the chosen option beside it takes. Verified
under `emulateMedia({ media: 'print' })`: `rgba(0,0,0,0)` fill, `#0369a1` ink
and border.

### Tests

`chooseFilingStatus(label)` (App.test.tsx:36) sets a status wherever the page
keeps it: it clicks the radio if there is one and drives the menu if there is
not. Every test that only needs the status *set* goes through it, so the next
move of a status between the two halves is one helper edit rather than fifteen.
The tests that are *about* the split address each half directly:

- `renders a filing status selector defaulting to Single` now also asserts the
  menu is showing its placeholder while the strip holds the answer.
- `keeps two statuses on the strip and the other two in the menu` reads the
  strip's values in order (`['single', 'mfj']`) and the menu's
  (`['', 'hoh', 'mfs']`), plus that the placeholder is disabled and says so in
  words. Written as the whole split because the failure worth catching is a
  status that has fallen off *both* halves.
- `hands the answer between the strip and the menu` holds the invariant the
  design rests on: with the menu holding it, no radio is checked and the recap
  still names the status; choosing from the strip takes it back and the menu
  returns to the placeholder.
- `offers Head of Household alongside the other three statuses` →
  `offers Head of Household from the menu beside the strip`.

`selectHoh` was a one-line wrapper around what is now the shared helper, so it
went and its six call sites call the helper.

