### Why this bullet came back

A human unchecked it after the run logged at line 7602. That run took the
three stacked `tspan`s out of the plot and rewrote `.chart-axis-label`, and
both of those hold: driven in Chromium this run, the chart's only `<text>`
nodes are `24.9K … 174.9K` and `0% … 24%`, and the caption reads
`Total income ($) including $24,852 of Social Security` — the second clause of
the bullet, verbatim.

What that run missed is that the phrase was in two places. The paragraph
directly under the slider — `.slider-readout`, App.tsx:1921 — opened
`<strong>You are here.</strong> At $30,000 of other income…`. In the
screenshot it sits about 160px below the plot with nothing between them but
the slider, which is close enough to "on graph" for a reader who does not know
the label used to be inside the axes. `body.innerText` on arrival still
matched /You are here/ exactly once, and this was it.

### What came off

Just the lead. The sentence already started with a capital `At`, so nothing
had to be rewritten around it:

```
-  <strong>You are here.</strong> At {formatCurrency(ordinaryIncome)} of
-  other income the next dollar is taxed at{' '}
+  At {formatCurrency(ordinaryIncome)} of other income the next
+  dollar is taxed at{' '}
```

The argument, left in a comment above the `<p>`: four things on this screen
already say the paragraph is about the reader's own point rather than the
chart's — the dashed amber marker, the amber slider directly above, the amber
figure beside the slider's label, and the sentence's own first five words,
which name the income the reader set. The label was the fifth, and because
`.slider-readout strong` is the styling the rate, the tax and the effective
rate all share, the phrase the paragraph stressed hardest was the only one
with no figure in it.

