## 2026-08-21 — Task: the Lines dropdown → Breakpoints, in the chart's corner

Three asks in one bullet: move the control off its own row, cut the panel to
two checkboxes with no explainer prose, and rename it.

### Starting state

The working tree was dirty on arrival — a previous run had begun this bullet
and not committed. Two of its three hunks were the shape of this work (the
`.chart-lines-lead` paragraph gone, the legend reworded) and one was not: it
also deleted the `.step-intro` paragraph above the chart, which is out of
scope here and is asserted on by five tests, including the whole `the torpedo
chart's right edge` block that reads the axis figures out of it
(App.test.tsx:1846). Reverted the file and redid the panel work from the
committed base.

### Where it went

**Not** over the plot, which was the first attempt and is wrong for this
chart. Overlaid at the container's top-right the button covered the `IRMAA 2`
and `IRMAA 3` reference-line labels outright and came within 2px of the curve
— this is a *marginal rate* curve, so it finishes near the top of the axis,
and its top-right is the least free corner it has. Measured in a real browser,
not reasoned about: labels at y 439±8, button at 397–431 in the overlay
version.

So the button takes a band of its own instead — `.chart-figure` gets
`position: relative` and `padding-top: 2.375rem` (the button's height and a
hair) and `.chart-lines` is absolute in its top-right corner. The plot starts
directly under it and the label band is clear by 4px, verified with a
bounding-box intersection over all four labels rather than by eye.

Height above the plot: 66px → 38px. The old cost was a full-width flex row
(34px) plus `.chart-container`'s 2rem gap, both spent on one small button.

