## 2026-08-21 — Task: Summary on bottom left should dynamically include the advanced inputs

Backlog 7, third bullet. Topmost unchecked was this one.

### What was wrong

`scenario-recap` (App.tsx, foot of step 1) named the year, the filing status,
the ages and the benefit, and then — only when an advanced input was off $0 —
appended `Plus whatever is set under Advanced inputs above.` That is a pointer
at a `<details>` that is shut by default, sitting in the one sentence on the
page whose entire job is to say which return is being priced. The figures were
already to hand: `advancedSet` (App.tsx:955) holds them, and the live region's
`benefit` reading (App.tsx:1274) already named them for exactly this reason —
with a comment saying it did so *because* the recap only pointed.

### The shape of the fix

Not "append a Plus sentence". The recap's sentence already runs on a
participle — "…, under 65, collecting $24,852 of Social Security a year." —
so the two inputs join that list rather than arriving behind a lead-in:

    …, collecting $24,852 of Social Security a year, holding $3,750 of
    tax-exempt interest, and giving $26,750 straight to charity out of an IRA.

The participles are the whole point and the one real decision here. A shared
"Plus" in front of both would be two additions, one of which is a
subtraction: "plus $26,750 given to charity" reads as $26,750 *of income*,
which is the opposite of what a QCD is. `collecting` / `holding` / `giving`
each carry their own direction and need nothing to share. There is a test
pinning that the recap contains no "plus" once a gift is set.

Wording follows the vocabulary the axis prose settled on last iteration
(`axisIncludes`, `axisCaption`): "of tax-exempt interest", "straight to
charity". The bullet's example says "municipal interest" and "qualified
charitable distributions", but those are the slider labels, not the page's
prose register, and the axis caption two inches below would then disagree
with the recap about what the same dollar is called.

### Code

- `listSeparator(i, n)` + `joinProse(parts)` + `<ProseList items>` — three
  small top-level helpers above `hereLine`. The list is rendered twice, as
  JSX with bolded figures and as flat text for the live region, and two
  hand-rolled joins would be two chances for the screen and the reading to
  disagree about a comma. One separator rule, two renderings.
- `advancedSet` entries gained `lead` and `tail` beside `label`. `label` is
  still the terse chip in the disclosure's own summary strip ("Muni interest
  $3,750 · Charitable $26,750"), which is a strip and wants labels; `lead`
  and `tail` are what the same fact is called in a sentence.
- `recapClauses` — the benefit clause plus one per set advanced input, as
  `{key, node}`. The benefit's own two-way branch (`$X of Social Security a
  year` / `no Social Security at all`) moved into it unchanged.
- The live region's `benefit` case now builds the same list through
  `joinProse` instead of tacking on `Muni interest $10,000`. Its no-benefit
  branch gained "at all", which is what the screen has always said.

### The lead sentence, left alone

The bullet's example opens "One year's return:" where the page says
"Everything from here on prices one return:". Left as is — the instruction is
that the summary should *include the advanced inputs*, and the current lead is
a deliberate choice with a comment above it explaining that the hero used to
carry the year and filing status and no longer does. Flagging rather than
silently rewriting: if the lead is wanted too, it is a one-line change.

### Tests

667 → 671. The single old test (`points at the advanced inputs only once one
has been set`) became five:

- names an advanced input once it is set, and only then — muni on, off again
- names the gift — QCD alone, two clauses, bare "and"
- runs both advanced inputs into the sentence it already had — the bullet's
  own example scenario, whole sentence pinned, serial comma
- does not add the gift on with a plus — `not.toMatch(/plus/i)`
- keeps the no-benefit reading a sentence when an input is set

Plus the live-region test rewritten to assert the reading matches the recap
and no longer says "Muni interest".

Mutation-checked twice: dropping the serial comma reddens 1 of 5; restoring
the old "Plus whatever is set…" pointer reddens all 5.

