## Text shouldn’t have meta references (21 Aug 2026)

The bullet quotes one sentence and says "wording like" it, so the task was the
register, not the sentence. Twelve sites, all of them the page as its own
subject: what it opens on, what it has no field for, why a curve is worth
drawing, which edge moves when a slider does.

### The rule I sorted them by

A reference to the chart is not automatically meta. "Nothing behind you on
this chart is cheaper than 22.2%" is only true of the range drawn — take
"on this chart" out and it becomes a claim about the whole return, which is
false. That is a scope qualifier and it stays. So does every instruction:
"hover the curve", "switch it on under Breakpoints", "(try the slider above)".
A reader using a control needs to be told where the control is.

What goes is the page narrating itself as a made thing — its drawing
decisions, its history, its missing fields:

- the QCD note's third sentence, the one the bullet quotes. Deleted whole; the
  note now ends at 408(d)(8)(A) and section 170(b), which is what a reader
  asking about a charitable distribution came for.
- the advanced-inputs note: "so the page opens on the plain picture" and the
  whole sentence about what stays set "for both of the steps that follow and is
