## Stop offering the two ways off the hump as equals (21 Aug 2026)

Backlog 7's last bullet. The peak branch of `StandingNote` closed with "the
move that pays is around this stretch rather than into it: stop short of the
near edge, or take enough at once to land past the far one" — two moves, one
sentence, no preference. The user wants the far edge recommended, and the
reason named: the rate is marginal.

### Why the far edge actually wins

Worth writing down because the sentence now asserts it. Hump runs $23,000 to
$41,000 at 22.2%; reader is standing at $30,000 and wants $50,000 more out of
an IRA. Taken $10,000 a year for five years, every one of those dollars lands
between the edges and is charged 22.2% — $11,100. Taken at once, the run from
$30,000 to $41,250 is charged 22.2% and the remaining $38,750 is charged 12%
and then 22% — because 22.2% is the price of the *next* dollar inside the
stretch, not the price of the income under it. Splitting the crossing costs
the same in hump-rate dollars and never arrives; that is the whole argument,
and it is the argument the 'climbing' branch two paragraphs down was already
making ("cheaper taken all at once, in one year that clears the hump, than a
slice at a time inside it"). The peak branch was the one place contradicting
it.

### What changed

The close is now three sentences: the marginal-rate fact, the consequence, the
instruction. It reuses `next.start` rather than minting a figure — the `clear`
clause above it already says "clearing $41,250 — $11,250 more — takes it to
12%", so the close points at that edge instead of re-deriving the distance.
Coming back under the near edge is still stated, in the `prev` clause where it
always was; what it no longer gets is equal billing in the recommendation.

Added a `next`-is-null arm, which the old flat sentence did not need. It cannot
fire on the drawn axis (the hump ends around $41,000 and the axis runs to
$178,602) but the branch above it already guards `next` for `clear`, so leaving
the close asserting a far edge that does not exist would have been the one way
this paragraph could lie.

