## Iteration — "clean up dead code" step 1: the render layer (Backlog 8)

Fourth clean-tree open running. The bullet says to break it into steps if
necessary, and it is: the sweep has two halves that want different decisions,
so this run did the first and wrote the second down as a sub-bullet rather
than guessing at it.

### What was actually dead

Six rules and four tokens, found by scanning selectors and `:root` against
what `App.tsx` can render:

- `.step-intro`, `.answer-share-line`, `.answer-share-line strong`,
  `.answer-note` — the four the previous run's Discovered Work already named.
- `.chart-key` and `.chart-key strong` — not on that list, and the better find.
  The paragraph of key under the plot went when the Breakpoints panel put each
  swatch beside the switch that draws its line; the comment above
  `.chart-key-swatch-subsidy` even says so ("the swatch outlived it") while two
  rules for the paragraph itself sat six lines above, unremarked.
- `--rose-rgb`, `--indigo-rgb`, `--lime-deep`, `--emerald-deep`, each in both
  the screen and the print `:root`.

The four tokens are the resolution of the `--lime-deep` bullet, taken the way
it framed the choice: a token earns its line by a `var()` or by a `PALETTE`
twin, and these have neither. `--indigo`, `--lime` and `--emerald` stay,
because `palette.ts` names them and says what they are held for — the
distinction is not "unspent" but "unaccounted".

### What was stale rather than dead

`index.css`'s shell comment listed the configuration column's inputs as "the
year, the status, the ages, the benefit, and the two advanced amounts" — the
year picker came off in Backlog 5 and the second advanced amount was the QCD
slider. Same comment: "a reader looking at step 3's chart", of a page with two
steps. `palette.ts` said "the three charts" and set the label size against "the
curve-in-words under each plot", which Backlog 5 deleted. And App.tsx's
`.answer-share` block still ended "The sentence is the feature and the button is
the convenience", of a sentence the manual text pass removed.

That last one is not only a comment. With the sentence gone the block is gated
entirely on `canCopyLink`, so a browser without `navigator.clipboard` renders
nothing there at all and never learns the address bar is the return. Logged
under Discovered work rather than fixed here: putting prose back that the user
deleted by hand is their call, and the alternative — render the button always,
let the failure message carry it — is a different edit than a comment.

### The two guards

Both halves of this were silent, which is the whole reason they accumulated,
so each got a test in `styles.test.tsx`:

- `writes no rule for a class the page never renders` — every class any
  selector mentions, against every class `App.tsx` emits. Read off source, not
  off a render, because the existing nesting check next to it *is* render-based
  and cannot tell "behind a closed panel" from "deleted": `.chart-key-swatch`
  and `.link-note` are both absent from a default render and both alive. Source
  works only because every `className` here is a literal string, so the test
  asserts that first (`expect(source).not.toMatch(/className=\{/)`) before
  relying on it. `recharts-*` is excluded and the comment says why — the print
  sheet re-colours the library's own SVG parts.
- `declares no colour with nothing on either side of it` — the existing palette
  test runs `PALETTE` → CSS only, which is exactly the direction `--lime-deep`
  fell through. This runs the other way.

Mutations, each reverted: a `.ghost-rule {}` appended to the stylesheet reddens
the first alone; restoring `--emerald-deep` reddens the second (and, usefully,
the print/screen parity test too, which is why the tokens had to come out of
both blocks).

One test changed rather than added. `the chart metrics` asserted `.chart-key`'s
font-size, which after this is a claim about a rule that does not exist, so its
`notes` list is down to `.chart-axis-label`. And `App.test.tsx:1523` asserted
`document.querySelector('.chart-key')` was null on arrival — true, but vacuously
so once nothing can render it; rewritten to `.chart-key-swatch`, which is a real
claim about a panel that starts shut.

